I'm going to introduce what the deep learning optimization

8
00:00:31,880 --> 00:00:36,640
problem looks like, which I think you already know.

9
00:00:36,640 --> 00:00:43,000
I want to cover some classical approaches towards optimization.

10
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:45,840
And these are things that, oftentimes, people

11
00:00:45,840 --> 00:00:48,780
in deep learning know about them and want them to work,

12
00:00:48,780 --> 00:00:52,920
but in practice, we don't use these things really

13
00:00:52,920 --> 00:00:56,082
to do deep learning optimization.

14
00:00:56,082 --> 00:00:58,540
So you want to introduce them, and how we think about them,

15
00:00:58,540 --> 00:01:02,130
and then think about maybe why we perhaps don't use them.

16
00:01:05,510 --> 00:01:08,070
And then towards the end of the lecture,

17
00:01:08,070 --> 00:01:11,190
I'll talk a bit about making the training really scalable

18
00:01:11,190 --> 00:01:12,910
and making the optimization algorithm

19
00:01:12,910 --> 00:01:14,830
scalable as you scale the architecture

20
00:01:14,830 --> 00:01:16,130
of your neural network.

21
00:01:18,990 --> 00:01:22,470
And then at the end, I just want to talk a little bit about--

22
00:01:22,470 --> 00:01:25,830
because this is of my research area-- also working on it

23
00:01:25,830 --> 00:01:28,125
with Phillip.

24
00:01:28,125 --> 00:01:29,750
So I want to just tell you a little bit

25
00:01:29,750 --> 00:01:31,875
about some of the things that we're thinking about.

26
00:01:31,875 --> 00:01:33,670
But you can be a little skeptical

27
00:01:33,670 --> 00:01:39,430
or ask the difficult questions, because that's

28
00:01:39,430 --> 00:01:40,330
what you should do.

29
00:01:43,790 --> 00:01:49,110
So this is a reminder that, so far, we've

30
00:01:49,110 --> 00:01:50,110
looked at this puzzle.

31
00:01:50,110 --> 00:01:53,230
And I think we've looked at the first piece of the puzzle

32
00:01:53,230 --> 00:01:58,580
approximation and the third one, generalization.

33
00:01:58,580 --> 00:02:00,060
So these were the questions of does

34
00:02:00,060 --> 00:02:03,180
there exist a neural network in my model family

35
00:02:03,180 --> 00:02:04,440
that fits the training data?

36
00:02:04,440 --> 00:02:06,980
If it does exist, can I find it?

37
00:02:06,980 --> 00:02:12,500
And does it do well on unseen data?

38
00:02:12,500 --> 00:02:18,340
But a perspective people have at the moment-- we could

39
00:02:18,340 --> 00:02:22,100
it may not be true, but just for the sake of argument.

40
00:02:22,100 --> 00:02:24,000
People are kind of like, approximation,

41
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:26,780
we've kind of solved that now because you just

42
00:02:26,780 --> 00:02:28,200
use a transformer for everything--

43
00:02:28,200 --> 00:02:30,060
really big transformer.

44
00:02:30,060 --> 00:02:31,780
Generalization we've kind of solved,

45
00:02:31,780 --> 00:02:34,420
because you just get so much data

46
00:02:34,420 --> 00:02:37,160
that everything generalizes.

47
00:02:37,160 --> 00:02:39,160
If you have the whole universe of training data,

48
00:02:39,160 --> 00:02:41,700
you'll never overfit.

49
00:02:41,700 --> 00:02:46,700
And something-- well, probably those

50
00:02:46,700 --> 00:02:49,180
are slightly too extreme positions, but just

51
00:02:49,180 --> 00:02:50,340
for the sake of argument.

52
00:02:50,340 --> 00:02:51,965
And for the sake of argument, the thing

53
00:02:51,965 --> 00:02:55,900
that we can improve upon is optimization.

54
00:02:55,900 --> 00:03:02,900
And this is the mindset if you're at a deep learning

55
00:03:02,900 --> 00:03:06,380
startup and you just want to make the optimization as

56
00:03:06,380 --> 00:03:08,780
efficient as possible, and then you just

57
00:03:08,780 --> 00:03:10,740
throw the big neural network at the problem

58
00:03:10,740 --> 00:03:12,200
and collect lots of data.

59
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:17,680
But maybe two-- what's the word-- caricature of a thing.

60
00:03:17,680 --> 00:03:20,380
But anyway, this lecture is just going

61
00:03:20,380 --> 00:03:22,760
to talk about the second question optimization.

62
00:03:26,140 --> 00:03:29,340
So I wanted to write it down in a formal way, where

63
00:03:29,340 --> 00:03:33,860
we have a neural network, which is a function f.

64
00:03:33,860 --> 00:03:38,820
And it takes inputs and weights, and it gives you an output.

65
00:03:38,820 --> 00:03:42,300
And we have an error measure, which I'll call L.

66
00:03:42,300 --> 00:03:47,140
And you think of this as cross-entropy or square error.

67
00:03:47,140 --> 00:03:51,380
And you think of y hat as your prediction and y as your target.

68
00:03:51,380 --> 00:03:53,740
And it somehow just measures the difference between them

69
00:03:53,740 --> 00:03:54,560
in some way.

70
00:03:57,650 --> 00:04:00,030
And then we collect here training data.

71
00:04:00,030 --> 00:04:03,370
So here, I'm thinking about paths of data

72
00:04:03,370 --> 00:04:06,410
with inputs and targets.

73
00:04:06,410 --> 00:04:12,250
And capital N is the number of data points in my data set.

74
00:04:12,250 --> 00:04:17,533
And then I'm going to write down an example of a classic machine

75
00:04:17,533 --> 00:04:19,450
learning or deep learning loss function, which

76
00:04:19,450 --> 00:04:24,130
is something like the average over the data set

77
00:04:24,130 --> 00:04:28,010
of-- let me just call it Li to be the error on the i-th data

78
00:04:28,010 --> 00:04:29,210
point.

79
00:04:29,210 --> 00:04:30,910
And Li would be--

80
00:04:35,570 --> 00:04:38,010
what that means-- it's shorthand for the loss

81
00:04:38,010 --> 00:04:46,330
between the output of the neural net on example i with weight w.

82
00:04:46,330 --> 00:04:50,470
And it's the discrepancy between that and the i-th target.

83
00:04:53,400 --> 00:04:56,760
So this is kind of what a loss function oftentimes

84
00:04:56,760 --> 00:05:00,560
looks like in machine learning.

85
00:05:00,560 --> 00:05:03,520
And you pick f-- these days, we pick f

86
00:05:03,520 --> 00:05:07,840
to be a really big transformer.

87
00:05:07,840 --> 00:05:10,960
N, we make it really, really large.

88
00:05:10,960 --> 00:05:13,800
And then we just try to find the W that

89
00:05:13,800 --> 00:05:17,400
minimizes this loss function.

90
00:05:17,400 --> 00:05:20,520
So there's two features I want to point out at this stage.

91
00:05:20,520 --> 00:05:22,000
One is that the loss function has

92
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:23,840
a kind of compositional structure, where

93
00:05:23,840 --> 00:05:27,860
we have an error measure and a neural network,

94
00:05:27,860 --> 00:05:29,380
and we glue them together.

95
00:05:29,380 --> 00:05:31,560
So there's two things that-- the error

96
00:05:31,560 --> 00:05:33,360
measure in the neural network and that

97
00:05:33,360 --> 00:05:34,460
composed with each other.

98
00:05:34,460 --> 00:05:38,240
So that's an interesting part of the loss function.

99
00:05:38,240 --> 00:05:44,520
And the other is that we average over the training set.

100
00:05:44,520 --> 00:05:46,380
And this is a picture of what we're doing.

101
00:05:46,380 --> 00:05:48,420
So we set up this loss function.

102
00:05:48,420 --> 00:05:53,050
And we start at some weight setting that has a large loss.

103
00:05:53,050 --> 00:05:55,190
And then we try to move the weights

104
00:05:55,190 --> 00:05:56,810
to the place with the small loss.

105
00:05:56,810 --> 00:06:00,310
And we think about going downhill on this.

106
00:06:00,310 --> 00:06:02,090
And we compute the gradients.

107
00:06:02,090 --> 00:06:04,110
And we try to get closer and closer,

108
00:06:04,110 --> 00:06:07,630
and eventually stop at this point hopefully.

109
00:06:07,630 --> 00:06:11,910
And so that's just saying that we iterate this gradient descent

110
00:06:11,910 --> 00:06:12,930
operation.

111
00:06:17,790 --> 00:06:20,030
Oops.

112
00:06:20,030 --> 00:06:22,270
Let's see.

113
00:06:22,270 --> 00:06:24,730
And this is the learning rate or the step size,

114
00:06:24,730 --> 00:06:25,890
which I just call eta.

115
00:06:25,890 --> 00:06:27,110
And this is the gradient.

116
00:06:30,070 --> 00:06:33,670
So what makes this hard-- in a sense,

117
00:06:33,670 --> 00:06:37,310
it's not hard, because we do it all the time,

118
00:06:37,310 --> 00:06:39,490
and we can train really big machine learning models.

119
00:06:39,490 --> 00:06:41,450
And it doesn't seem to be-- we can do it.

120
00:06:41,450 --> 00:06:45,750
But what are some of the things which make this

121
00:06:45,750 --> 00:06:47,830
like challenging in a way--

122
00:06:47,830 --> 00:06:53,900
some of the examples are There are lots of weights.

123
00:06:53,900 --> 00:06:58,740
It's really high-dimensional optimization.

124
00:06:58,740 --> 00:07:02,740
Another thing is the neural network may be very deep.

125
00:07:02,740 --> 00:07:05,857
It has lots of layers, and that can make--

126
00:07:05,857 --> 00:07:07,940
for a while, we couldn't train really, really deep

127
00:07:07,940 --> 00:07:08,720
neural networks.

128
00:07:08,720 --> 00:07:11,000
But with some advances, now we can.

129
00:07:13,860 --> 00:07:15,320
What else is hard?

130
00:07:15,320 --> 00:07:16,600
There's lots of data.

131
00:07:19,140 --> 00:07:24,360
So usually, we do stochastic gradients.

132
00:07:24,360 --> 00:07:27,000
We compute gradients on a handful of data points.

133
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:28,460
And then on the next iteration, we

134
00:07:28,460 --> 00:07:30,660
pick another handful of data points.

135
00:07:30,660 --> 00:07:33,460
So that means that we just have noisy estimates

136
00:07:33,460 --> 00:07:38,047
of the full gradient across all the data points.

137
00:07:38,047 --> 00:07:40,380
For the purpose of this lecture, I actually kind of want

138
00:07:40,380 --> 00:07:42,020
to ignore this problem.

139
00:07:42,020 --> 00:07:45,260
And these two first problems are already

140
00:07:45,260 --> 00:07:49,610
kind of-- if you just think about those ones,

141
00:07:49,610 --> 00:07:51,730
there's really a lot of interesting things to say

142
00:07:51,730 --> 00:07:53,130
and things to think about.

143
00:07:53,130 --> 00:07:57,470
So in other words, for the sake of this lecture,

144
00:07:57,470 --> 00:08:02,370
let's just assume that we're in the full batch setting, i.e.,

145
00:08:02,370 --> 00:08:05,690
we can evaluate the true gradient of the whole loss

146
00:08:05,690 --> 00:08:07,590
function on all the data at every step.

147
00:08:07,590 --> 00:08:09,350
Let's just assume that we can do that.

148
00:08:18,120 --> 00:08:20,470
So towards the end of the lecture,

149
00:08:20,470 --> 00:08:23,050
I want to talk a little bit about some of the problems

150
00:08:23,050 --> 00:08:24,590
that we have when we do scaling--

151
00:08:24,590 --> 00:08:27,330
when we scale up or scale up our neural network.

152
00:08:27,330 --> 00:08:30,850
So I want you to think that this is a neural network where

153
00:08:30,850 --> 00:08:36,049
I pass in an input here, and I get the output.

154
00:08:36,049 --> 00:08:39,070
And I'm either going to be interested in scaling the width,

155
00:08:39,070 --> 00:08:42,450
like making the network wider, or potentially scaling the depth

156
00:08:42,450 --> 00:08:45,090
and making the network deeper.

157
00:08:45,090 --> 00:08:48,130
And we kind of run into two nuisances

158
00:08:48,130 --> 00:08:52,610
when we do this if you do it naively.

159
00:08:52,610 --> 00:08:54,450
And the first one is actually a little bit

160
00:08:54,450 --> 00:08:57,890
explored in this homework.

161
00:08:57,890 --> 00:09:01,650
So this is a plot of training loss.

162
00:09:01,650 --> 00:09:04,850
And I'm training networks with a range of learning rates

163
00:09:04,850 --> 00:09:06,530
on the x-axis.

164
00:09:06,530 --> 00:09:10,310
And the different curves are increasing width.

165
00:09:10,310 --> 00:09:15,210
So as I go down, it's getting wider.

166
00:09:15,210 --> 00:09:17,130
And what you typically see is that if you make

167
00:09:17,130 --> 00:09:21,290
your neural network wider, you see that the best loss you

168
00:09:21,290 --> 00:09:24,050
achieve, you see it kind of--

169
00:09:24,050 --> 00:09:26,310
you get a better loss as you make the network wider,

170
00:09:26,310 --> 00:09:27,090
which is good.

171
00:09:27,090 --> 00:09:29,370
That's why people want to scale their network,

172
00:09:29,370 --> 00:09:32,970
because it improves the performance.

173
00:09:32,970 --> 00:09:37,970
But you see that on the other hand, if you

174
00:09:37,970 --> 00:09:43,490
look at the learning rate, which gets the best loss, it changes.

175
00:09:43,490 --> 00:09:46,140
As I scale my model, the best learning rate changes.

176
00:09:46,140 --> 00:09:49,382
So that means like, typically, if you train a neural network,

177
00:09:49,382 --> 00:09:51,340
you don't run this full sweep of learning rate.

178
00:09:51,340 --> 00:09:52,680
You just pick a learning rate, and then

179
00:09:52,680 --> 00:09:54,120
maybe you try to scale your model,

180
00:09:54,120 --> 00:09:56,120
and you try to run the training again.

181
00:09:56,120 --> 00:09:58,940
And you find, oh, my training isn't working.

182
00:09:58,940 --> 00:10:02,060
Because just because I made my neural network bigger.

183
00:10:02,060 --> 00:10:04,280
But then you have to retune the learning rate

184
00:10:04,280 --> 00:10:07,280
at the large scale.

185
00:10:07,280 --> 00:10:09,000
So this is just like a kind of nuisance

186
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:12,000
that, as we scale the whole lost kind of landscape

187
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:15,040
as a function of step size is kind of drifting.

188
00:10:15,040 --> 00:10:16,060
That's a nuisance.

189
00:10:16,060 --> 00:10:18,400
And similarly, if we scale depth,

190
00:10:18,400 --> 00:10:20,660
and we make the network deeper-- so it's the same.

191
00:10:20,660 --> 00:10:23,920
The different curves here are, as I go down

192
00:10:23,920 --> 00:10:26,120
it's increasing depth.

193
00:10:26,120 --> 00:10:29,455
And we're plotting training loss against learning rate.

194
00:10:29,455 --> 00:10:31,580
And something you can see-- if you do this naively,

195
00:10:31,580 --> 00:10:36,120
you just find that performance gets worse as I make my model

196
00:10:36,120 --> 00:10:38,080
deeper, which is--

197
00:10:38,080 --> 00:10:40,480
that's undesirable.

198
00:10:40,480 --> 00:10:42,592
Yeah?

199
00:10:42,592 --> 00:10:44,550
AUDIENCE: Isn't that the optimal learning rate?

200
00:10:44,550 --> 00:10:48,250
Because it looks like the training loss kind of drifts,

201
00:10:48,250 --> 00:10:49,730
but it maintains the same shape.

202
00:10:49,730 --> 00:10:54,350
So the optimal learning rate and finding

203
00:10:54,350 --> 00:10:57,507
that minimum be the same [INAUDIBLE] loss exists.

204
00:10:57,507 --> 00:10:59,590
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah, let's just pick two points

205
00:10:59,590 --> 00:11:00,610
and just compare them.

206
00:11:00,610 --> 00:11:04,950
So let's say that we're at width 32.

207
00:11:04,950 --> 00:11:07,770
The best loss is about here on the dark blue curve.

208
00:11:07,770 --> 00:11:11,010
And that corresponds to this learning rate,

209
00:11:11,010 --> 00:11:15,110
which is if we're at a larger width, 1,024,

210
00:11:15,110 --> 00:11:16,450
the best loss occurs here.

211
00:11:16,450 --> 00:11:18,658
And it just corresponds to a different learning rate.

212
00:11:18,658 --> 00:11:21,270
So it's the fact that the minima occur at different points

213
00:11:21,270 --> 00:11:23,030
on the x-axis.

214
00:11:23,030 --> 00:11:27,070
That's what I'm trying to point out.

215
00:11:27,070 --> 00:11:29,430
Yeah?

216
00:11:29,430 --> 00:11:31,070
AUDIENCE: Do we have any intuition

217
00:11:31,070 --> 00:11:34,558
of why the optimal learning rate changes as we scale the width.

218
00:11:34,558 --> 00:11:36,350
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah, that's at the end--

219
00:11:36,350 --> 00:11:39,810
towards the end of the lecture, I'll try to explain why.

220
00:11:42,380 --> 00:11:43,900
Oh, yeah?

221
00:11:43,900 --> 00:11:45,340
AUDIENCE: So I'm just curious--

222
00:11:45,340 --> 00:11:48,120
in practice, people use deeper neural networks.

223
00:11:48,120 --> 00:11:50,520
But if you're just looking at this graph here,

224
00:11:50,520 --> 00:11:52,900
you would not use one because the loss

225
00:11:52,900 --> 00:11:54,720
is worse if [INAUDIBLE].

226
00:11:54,720 --> 00:11:56,700
So how do you reconcile?

227
00:11:56,700 --> 00:12:00,020
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah, so this is basically pre, let's say,

228
00:12:00,020 --> 00:12:02,920
2015.

229
00:12:02,920 --> 00:12:06,880
The max depth people went to was, let's say, 16 or something.

230
00:12:06,880 --> 00:12:08,760
And then people figured out techniques.

231
00:12:08,760 --> 00:12:11,580
And now, you can train a network with thousands of layers.

232
00:12:11,580 --> 00:12:14,300
So I'm just saying if you do things very naively-- if you

233
00:12:14,300 --> 00:12:16,115
don't know that much about deep learning,

234
00:12:16,115 --> 00:12:18,240
this is like-- you would see this type of behavior.

235
00:12:18,240 --> 00:12:19,800
Then, as you learn more and more,

236
00:12:19,800 --> 00:12:21,842
then you know how to fix these problems.

237
00:12:21,842 --> 00:12:23,300
So I'll talk a little bit about how

238
00:12:23,300 --> 00:12:24,820
to fix some of these problems.

239
00:12:24,820 --> 00:12:27,445
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] with those fixes, that you can bring loss

240
00:12:27,445 --> 00:12:28,420
back down [INAUDIBLE].

241
00:12:28,420 --> 00:12:30,700
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yes, essentially, the fix for depth

242
00:12:30,700 --> 00:12:37,020
is use a residual architecture and set up the residual blocks

243
00:12:37,020 --> 00:12:38,100
in a good way.

244
00:12:38,100 --> 00:12:40,060
And you could use a residual architecture

245
00:12:40,060 --> 00:12:42,150
and set up the residual blocks badly,

246
00:12:42,150 --> 00:12:44,030
and you'd still see this kind of behavior,

247
00:12:44,030 --> 00:12:46,450
or set up the interaction between the optimization

248
00:12:46,450 --> 00:12:48,110
algorithm and the residual blocks.

249
00:12:48,110 --> 00:12:50,910
So if you solve at least enough of the problems,

250
00:12:50,910 --> 00:12:52,710
you can fix this behavior.

251
00:12:52,710 --> 00:12:54,150
And if you solve all the problems,

252
00:12:54,150 --> 00:12:58,210
then you can fix it in a really nice way where everything--

253
00:12:58,210 --> 00:13:00,830
the optimal learning rate always transfers across scale

254
00:13:00,830 --> 00:13:01,452
and so on.

255
00:13:01,452 --> 00:13:03,410
AUDIENCE: And that's the same on the left where

256
00:13:03,410 --> 00:13:06,332
you can eliminate the drift?

257
00:13:06,332 --> 00:13:07,290
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah.

258
00:13:07,290 --> 00:13:08,850
The point on the left is that even

259
00:13:08,850 --> 00:13:16,850
with all the latest fixes prior to 2021, it had that drift.

260
00:13:16,850 --> 00:13:20,710
But then if you know all the literature from 2021 until now,

261
00:13:20,710 --> 00:13:22,350
then you can fix it for sure.

262
00:13:25,450 --> 00:13:28,290
So anyway, these are just some examples of some problems

263
00:13:28,290 --> 00:13:30,570
how things can go badly when we scale.

264
00:13:30,570 --> 00:13:33,910
And if you're trying to scale massive transformers,

265
00:13:33,910 --> 00:13:36,370
you may not even have the resources

266
00:13:36,370 --> 00:13:39,190
to tune all the hyperparameters of a really big model.

267
00:13:39,190 --> 00:13:41,240
So you may be like in this regime

268
00:13:41,240 --> 00:13:43,360
where you can only tune things at small scale

269
00:13:43,360 --> 00:13:44,740
and then try to transfer them.

270
00:13:44,740 --> 00:13:46,520
So that's one of the applications

271
00:13:46,520 --> 00:13:48,380
of fixing this type of problem.

272
00:13:55,960 --> 00:13:58,860
That was just a kind of teaser for the end of the lecture.

273
00:13:58,860 --> 00:14:01,760
Now, I want to just-- let's go back to basics and just

274
00:14:01,760 --> 00:14:05,120
how should we think about designing optimization

275
00:14:05,120 --> 00:14:09,920
algorithms and solving optimization problems?

276
00:14:09,920 --> 00:14:15,960
So I want to look at a couple of different types of methods.

277
00:14:15,960 --> 00:14:18,160
The first is first order methods, which

278
00:14:18,160 --> 00:14:20,360
only use first derivatives.

279
00:14:20,360 --> 00:14:22,620
So they basically only use gradients,

280
00:14:22,620 --> 00:14:24,120
which if you think about it, is kind

281
00:14:24,120 --> 00:14:27,280
of what we do in deep learning.

282
00:14:27,280 --> 00:14:29,320
And I'm going to use this symbol g

283
00:14:29,320 --> 00:14:31,800
as a shorthand for the derivative of the loss

284
00:14:31,800 --> 00:14:33,880
with respect to its weights.

285
00:14:33,880 --> 00:14:37,360
So g is always going to mean the gradient

286
00:14:37,360 --> 00:14:39,720
of the loss with respect to the weights, i.e., the thing

287
00:14:39,720 --> 00:14:43,480
that you get by doing back propagation.

288
00:14:43,480 --> 00:14:45,460
And then there's also second order methods

289
00:14:45,460 --> 00:14:47,700
which, in addition to first derivatives,

290
00:14:47,700 --> 00:14:51,120
they also use the second derivatives of the loss

291
00:14:51,120 --> 00:14:51,940
function.

292
00:14:54,720 --> 00:14:57,866
So they're called second order methods.

293
00:14:57,866 --> 00:15:04,680
And this symbol h is going to be a shorthand

294
00:15:04,680 --> 00:15:07,000
for the second derivatives of the loss with respect

295
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:08,160
to the weights.

296
00:15:08,160 --> 00:15:10,300
So it's a big matrix of second derivatives.

297
00:15:10,300 --> 00:15:13,480
So you can think of the gradient as being one big vector.

298
00:15:13,480 --> 00:15:17,080
The Hessian is a matrix of second derivatives corresponding

299
00:15:17,080 --> 00:15:19,260
to all pairs of second derivatives.

300
00:15:22,320 --> 00:15:28,280
And the thing that I hope to get at

301
00:15:28,280 --> 00:15:32,240
is to explain the modeling assumptions

302
00:15:32,240 --> 00:15:37,630
that different approaches make and then

303
00:15:37,630 --> 00:15:43,750
also think about potential shortcomings of those modeling

304
00:15:43,750 --> 00:15:44,730
assumptions.

305
00:15:48,950 --> 00:15:51,890
So you should always ask-- if I present something to you,

306
00:15:51,890 --> 00:15:54,050
you should always say, well, why don't we do this?

307
00:15:54,050 --> 00:15:57,190
Why don't we actually do this in practice?

308
00:15:57,190 --> 00:15:58,970
That should be the question that you have.

309
00:16:03,220 --> 00:16:06,870
So all of these different methods, I would argue,

310
00:16:06,870 --> 00:16:10,990
start by Taylor expanding the loss function.

311
00:16:10,990 --> 00:16:13,230
So different classical approaches to optimization

312
00:16:13,230 --> 00:16:15,710
take the Taylor expansion as a starting point.

313
00:16:15,710 --> 00:16:19,830
And I'm writing this Taylor expansion like this.

314
00:16:19,830 --> 00:16:21,750
But because we introduce the shorthand,

315
00:16:21,750 --> 00:16:25,430
I can rewrite it like this.

316
00:16:25,430 --> 00:16:32,310
So the first order term is gradient inner product delta w.

317
00:16:32,310 --> 00:16:35,460
And then the second order term is--

318
00:16:35,460 --> 00:16:37,320
it's this Hessian term.

319
00:16:41,820 --> 00:16:43,940
So the Taylor expansion up to second order

320
00:16:43,940 --> 00:16:45,320
is like a quadratic form.

321
00:16:45,320 --> 00:16:50,180
And then it's like a multivariate Taylor expansion.

322
00:16:50,180 --> 00:16:53,180
So again, this is the loss evaluated

323
00:16:53,180 --> 00:16:54,820
at a kind of perturbed weight vector,

324
00:16:54,820 --> 00:16:57,340
or weight vector plus a delta w.

325
00:16:57,340 --> 00:17:01,020
And then we can just write out the Taylor expansion.

326
00:17:01,020 --> 00:17:03,400
And I want to give-- these two terms,

327
00:17:03,400 --> 00:17:04,680
I want to give them a name.

328
00:17:04,680 --> 00:17:08,380
And we'll call them the linearization

329
00:17:08,380 --> 00:17:09,520
of our loss function.

330
00:17:09,520 --> 00:17:12,940
And then all the higher order things

331
00:17:12,940 --> 00:17:15,359
we'll call the nonlinear part.

332
00:17:18,900 --> 00:17:21,440
And again, we're using this shorthand.

333
00:17:21,440 --> 00:17:27,460
The gradient is this, and the Hessian matrix is this thing.

334
00:17:27,460 --> 00:17:32,080
And I just wanted to just visually give you a picture.

335
00:17:32,080 --> 00:17:34,790
So the gradient we can think of as this big vector.

336
00:17:38,770 --> 00:17:41,710
And the Hessian we can think of as a big matrix.

337
00:17:44,530 --> 00:17:47,410
If there are d parameters in the whole neural network--

338
00:17:47,410 --> 00:17:53,050
the size of the weight space is like d dimensions,

339
00:17:53,050 --> 00:17:59,170
the gradient is d by 1, and the Hessian is d by d.

340
00:17:59,170 --> 00:18:01,310
So it's a d by d matrix.

341
00:18:04,650 --> 00:18:09,010
So our first method that I want to talk about

342
00:18:09,010 --> 00:18:10,970
is called Newton's method.

343
00:18:10,970 --> 00:18:14,170
Do people already know--

344
00:18:14,170 --> 00:18:17,970
is it something that people have heard about?

345
00:18:17,970 --> 00:18:19,870
Well, let's just quickly talk about it.

346
00:18:19,870 --> 00:18:24,622
So the idea of Newton's method is we kind of drop--

347
00:18:24,622 --> 00:18:27,330
or remember there were all those higher order terms in the Taylor

348
00:18:27,330 --> 00:18:27,830
expansion.

349
00:18:27,830 --> 00:18:29,530
We just kind of forget about them.

350
00:18:29,530 --> 00:18:34,440
Let's just ignore them and take the Taylor expansion up

351
00:18:34,440 --> 00:18:35,680
to first order.

352
00:18:35,680 --> 00:18:38,640
And then we're going to think about picking our optimization

353
00:18:38,640 --> 00:18:41,920
step as choosing a delta w.

354
00:18:41,920 --> 00:18:43,980
We think of w as our current weights.

355
00:18:43,980 --> 00:18:46,768
And we're going to pick a delta w to add to them.

356
00:18:46,768 --> 00:18:48,560
And the way we're going to do that is we're

357
00:18:48,560 --> 00:18:52,960
going to minimize this quadratic form with respect to the weight

358
00:18:52,960 --> 00:18:55,800
perturbation delta w.

359
00:18:55,800 --> 00:19:00,560
Wait, does anyone know how do I minimize that quadratic form?

360
00:19:00,560 --> 00:19:06,160
Can someone tell me how do I do it?

361
00:19:06,160 --> 00:19:08,320
AUDIENCE: Take the derivative of [INAUDIBLE].

362
00:19:08,320 --> 00:19:08,960
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah--

363
00:19:08,960 --> 00:19:10,377
AUDIENCE: That's what [INAUDIBLE].

364
00:19:11,417 --> 00:19:12,500
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Exactly.

365
00:19:12,500 --> 00:19:14,160
So we differentiate it with respect

366
00:19:14,160 --> 00:19:22,440
to delta w which gives me lambda over 2 times 2, h delta w.

367
00:19:22,440 --> 00:19:23,980
And then these cancel.

368
00:19:23,980 --> 00:19:26,360
And then we set the derivative to 0.

369
00:19:26,360 --> 00:19:30,870
That's that thing that we all did once upon-- yeah.

370
00:19:30,870 --> 00:19:33,990
And then we just solve for delta w by rearranging,

371
00:19:33,990 --> 00:19:37,110
and we get like this thing.

372
00:19:37,110 --> 00:19:39,210
And we just call this Newton's method.

373
00:19:44,230 --> 00:19:48,150
And people describe this as you get your gradient.

374
00:19:48,150 --> 00:19:50,310
And then they talk about preconditioning it

375
00:19:50,310 --> 00:19:52,012
with the inverse Hessian.

376
00:19:52,012 --> 00:19:54,470
But it basically means you take the Hessian, you invert it,

377
00:19:54,470 --> 00:19:56,590
and then you multiply it with the gradient.

378
00:19:56,590 --> 00:19:59,190
And we call this Newton's method for--

379
00:19:59,190 --> 00:20:03,070
it's an optimization algorithm that uses first and second order

380
00:20:03,070 --> 00:20:06,430
information.

381
00:20:06,430 --> 00:20:09,990
So I just rewrote that here.

382
00:20:09,990 --> 00:20:12,990
So what are the problems?

383
00:20:12,990 --> 00:20:14,750
Why don't we actually use Newton's method

384
00:20:14,750 --> 00:20:16,710
to train neural networks?

385
00:20:16,710 --> 00:20:17,890
What are the--

386
00:20:17,890 --> 00:20:20,790
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

387
00:20:20,790 --> 00:20:21,950
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah.

388
00:20:21,950 --> 00:20:27,990
So problem one is that the Hessian is d by d matrix.

389
00:20:27,990 --> 00:20:32,810
And d may be billions in a large neural network,

390
00:20:32,810 --> 00:20:36,110
so you can't even store such a large matrix.

391
00:20:36,110 --> 00:20:38,470
And if you could, you could invert it.

392
00:20:38,470 --> 00:20:40,610
And you just basically-- it's just too big.

393
00:20:43,910 --> 00:20:46,070
Another problem with Newton's method

394
00:20:46,070 --> 00:20:50,750
is, if you think about how we derived it--

395
00:20:50,750 --> 00:20:53,030
are we sure that this procedure is giving us

396
00:20:53,030 --> 00:20:56,150
a minimum of the quadratic form?

397
00:20:56,150 --> 00:20:58,822
Do we know-- all we did is--

398
00:20:58,822 --> 00:21:00,350
AUDIENCE: It's one level.

399
00:21:00,350 --> 00:21:02,225
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: It's just the first-- yeah,

400
00:21:02,225 --> 00:21:03,670
so what do you mean?

401
00:21:03,670 --> 00:21:05,990
Could it be a minimum, or could it be something else?

402
00:21:05,990 --> 00:21:07,310
It could be a maximum.

403
00:21:07,310 --> 00:21:11,350
It's just finding a critical point of the quadratic form.

404
00:21:11,350 --> 00:21:15,170
If it's close to a max, it could find a maximum.

405
00:21:15,170 --> 00:21:18,050
So it's not even necessarily doing gradient descent.

406
00:21:18,050 --> 00:21:21,070
It could be doing gradient ascent.

407
00:21:21,070 --> 00:21:23,990
And there are ways to fix these type of things.

408
00:21:23,990 --> 00:21:26,170
There's something called cubic regularization,

409
00:21:26,170 --> 00:21:29,400
which adds a cubic penalty to the quadratic form.

410
00:21:32,040 --> 00:21:34,500
But people don't use that.

411
00:21:34,500 --> 00:21:36,160
And any time we present a method,

412
00:21:36,160 --> 00:21:39,420
there's always like attempts to make it practical.

413
00:21:39,420 --> 00:21:42,820
And like there's a literature on that method.

414
00:21:42,820 --> 00:21:45,460
But then in practice, we just use

415
00:21:45,460 --> 00:21:47,200
Adam to train neural networks.

416
00:21:50,140 --> 00:21:54,180
So that's Newton's method.

417
00:21:54,180 --> 00:21:55,820
Now, we're going to look at something

418
00:21:55,820 --> 00:21:59,922
called Gauss-Newton method, which is a little bit different.

419
00:21:59,922 --> 00:22:01,380
And it starts with something called

420
00:22:01,380 --> 00:22:04,700
the Gauss-Newton decomposition.

421
00:22:04,700 --> 00:22:10,020
So here, we suppose that we have a composite objective function.

422
00:22:10,020 --> 00:22:13,380
It's the composition of two functions, which is-- remember,

423
00:22:13,380 --> 00:22:15,140
we actually have that in machine learning,

424
00:22:15,140 --> 00:22:17,280
because we have an error, and we have a neural net.

425
00:22:17,280 --> 00:22:19,640
So we're actually in this situation.

426
00:22:22,340 --> 00:22:24,420
That's what this is saying.

427
00:22:24,420 --> 00:22:29,110
So let's derive the Gauss-Newton decomposition.

428
00:22:29,110 --> 00:22:33,450
So we start with the gradient, which is dL by dw.

429
00:22:33,450 --> 00:22:37,130
Because we have a composite we can do the chain rule.

430
00:22:37,130 --> 00:22:43,250
So we get dL by df, df by dw.

431
00:22:43,250 --> 00:22:44,790
So that's just the chain rule.

432
00:22:48,650 --> 00:22:51,210
And now, let's say we want to know about the Hessian.

433
00:22:51,210 --> 00:22:53,290
This is the second derivative of the loss

434
00:22:53,290 --> 00:22:54,970
with respect to the weights.

435
00:22:54,970 --> 00:22:57,730
Well, now, we just need to differentiate the gradient

436
00:22:57,730 --> 00:22:58,810
again.

437
00:22:58,810 --> 00:23:01,310
But we recognize now that we have a product.

438
00:23:01,310 --> 00:23:04,390
So we can do like the product rule.

439
00:23:08,640 --> 00:23:13,410
And let me just remember how to do the product rule.

440
00:23:13,410 --> 00:23:25,360
d-- so this is like the derivative

441
00:23:25,360 --> 00:23:27,160
of this with respect to w, and then

442
00:23:27,160 --> 00:23:31,120
one application of the chain rule.

443
00:23:31,120 --> 00:23:35,580
And then there's this term, which is just like this term.

444
00:23:35,580 --> 00:23:37,920
And I haven't differentiated it.

445
00:23:37,920 --> 00:23:41,260
And then we have to do the second thing, which is--

446
00:23:46,538 --> 00:23:48,080
like, I don't differentiate this one,

447
00:23:48,080 --> 00:23:49,960
and I do differentiate this one.

448
00:23:49,960 --> 00:23:59,420
So I get dl by df, D squared f by dw squared.

449
00:23:59,420 --> 00:24:01,960
And so what I hope to have shown you

450
00:24:01,960 --> 00:24:04,540
is that if you just have a composition of two things,

451
00:24:04,540 --> 00:24:06,880
you can derive a formula for the second derivative,

452
00:24:06,880 --> 00:24:10,340
and it splits up into two pieces because of the product rule.

453
00:24:10,340 --> 00:24:14,000
And we just call that the Gauss-Newton decomposition.

454
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:15,180
So I wrote it out.

455
00:24:18,120 --> 00:24:22,230
So what this is saying is that the full Hessian of a composite

456
00:24:22,230 --> 00:24:25,790
decomposes into these two pieces.

457
00:24:25,790 --> 00:24:28,327
And we think if we think of this as being the curvature,

458
00:24:28,327 --> 00:24:29,910
it's like the second order information

459
00:24:29,910 --> 00:24:32,542
about the whole objective function,

460
00:24:32,542 --> 00:24:34,750
you can break it up into the second order information

461
00:24:34,750 --> 00:24:38,328
about the error with respect to the model,

462
00:24:38,328 --> 00:24:40,870
and the second order information about the model with respect

463
00:24:40,870 --> 00:24:41,950
to the weights.

464
00:24:41,950 --> 00:24:44,870
So it's like decomposing curvature

465
00:24:44,870 --> 00:24:47,830
into one piece from the error and one piece from the model.

466
00:24:47,830 --> 00:24:51,110
That's called Gauss-Newton decomposition.

467
00:24:51,110 --> 00:24:53,570
So then what people do is they say,

468
00:24:53,570 --> 00:24:57,310
let's pretend that our error measure is the squared error.

469
00:24:57,310 --> 00:24:58,970
And we know that for the squared error,

470
00:24:58,970 --> 00:25:01,030
the second derivative with respect to the model

471
00:25:01,030 --> 00:25:06,190
is just basically one or some-- yeah.

472
00:25:06,190 --> 00:25:09,790
So then, basically, we can drop this term.

473
00:25:09,790 --> 00:25:12,750
We just say that that's 1.

474
00:25:12,750 --> 00:25:14,950
And then they say, let's just ignore

475
00:25:14,950 --> 00:25:21,180
the curvature of our model, and let's just ignore this term.

476
00:25:21,180 --> 00:25:22,920
It's just an assumption that people make.

477
00:25:22,920 --> 00:25:26,880
They say, hey, I don't really know how to deal with this term,

478
00:25:26,880 --> 00:25:28,200
so let's just ignore it.

479
00:25:33,940 --> 00:25:38,420
So under those like steps, h which is d squared,

480
00:25:38,420 --> 00:25:44,700
l by dw squared, is just equal to somehow the product of df

481
00:25:44,700 --> 00:25:47,980
by dw and df by dw.

482
00:25:47,980 --> 00:25:49,580
So then, if I want to do a Newton

483
00:25:49,580 --> 00:25:54,300
method but using this form of the Hessian,

484
00:25:54,300 --> 00:25:56,200
what does Newton's method look like?

485
00:25:56,200 --> 00:26:04,300
It's delta w is minus h inverse g, which is now minus df

486
00:26:04,300 --> 00:26:09,740
by dw df by dw inverse g.

487
00:26:12,700 --> 00:26:15,380
If you go through this, you'll realize that all of these things

488
00:26:15,380 --> 00:26:18,080
are tensors and you need to be careful about all the indexing.

489
00:26:18,080 --> 00:26:23,620
But that's something to just check.

490
00:26:23,620 --> 00:26:27,580
And we call this thing the Gauss-Newton, method

491
00:26:27,580 --> 00:26:29,220
because it uses-- it started with

492
00:26:29,220 --> 00:26:32,900
the Gauss-Newton decomposition.

493
00:26:32,900 --> 00:26:34,820
And it's just another way of deriving

494
00:26:34,820 --> 00:26:41,340
an algorithm which looks some kind of iterative weight update.

495
00:26:41,340 --> 00:26:44,060
And I've just rewritten it here.

496
00:26:44,060 --> 00:26:47,200
So this is thing that we're calling the Gauss-Newton method.

497
00:26:47,200 --> 00:26:49,140
And remember, these are the derivatives

498
00:26:49,140 --> 00:26:51,240
of the model with respect to the weights.

499
00:26:51,240 --> 00:26:54,100
So it's not the same thing as the regular gradient

500
00:26:54,100 --> 00:26:55,440
that you usually think about.

501
00:26:55,440 --> 00:26:56,680
It's a different gradient.

502
00:27:00,460 --> 00:27:05,460
And this is just to remind you about the assumptions

503
00:27:05,460 --> 00:27:06,740
that the method made.

504
00:27:06,740 --> 00:27:08,980
One is just drop this term, and the other one

505
00:27:08,980 --> 00:27:12,540
is like set this one to 1.

506
00:27:12,540 --> 00:27:15,140
So what do people think of-- what's the problem?

507
00:27:15,140 --> 00:27:16,840
Why don't I actually do this?

508
00:27:19,910 --> 00:27:21,410
AUDIENCE: For deep learning, you may

509
00:27:21,410 --> 00:27:24,652
have a low rank Gaussian approximation.

510
00:27:24,652 --> 00:27:26,610
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Oh, so that could be a reason

511
00:27:26,610 --> 00:27:28,470
why I would want to do it.

512
00:27:28,470 --> 00:27:29,870
Is that what you're saying?

513
00:27:29,870 --> 00:27:31,495
AUDIENCE: No, why you wouldn't want to.

514
00:27:31,495 --> 00:27:33,787
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Oh, because you're saying if it's low

515
00:27:33,787 --> 00:27:34,790
rank, I can't invert it?

516
00:27:37,350 --> 00:27:42,090
Yeah, I need to think more about that, but you might be right.

517
00:27:42,090 --> 00:27:43,730
Oftentimes, people will always just--

518
00:27:43,730 --> 00:27:45,110
if they want to invert something,

519
00:27:45,110 --> 00:27:47,090
they'll add a little bit of identity matrix

520
00:27:47,090 --> 00:27:49,373
