779
00:40:59,770 --> 00:41:01,910
And even though it's a general norm,

780
00:41:01,910 --> 00:41:04,410
we can still actually say something about it.

781
00:41:04,410 --> 00:41:06,250
And what you can show, and I think

782
00:41:06,250 --> 00:41:10,310
this is also on the homework, is to show that it--

783
00:41:10,310 --> 00:41:14,570
the solution to this thing can be transformed

784
00:41:14,570 --> 00:41:18,130
into this other thing.

785
00:41:18,130 --> 00:41:20,410
And we can think of this as like-- we

786
00:41:20,410 --> 00:41:24,710
think of this as being the step size or the learning rate.

787
00:41:27,970 --> 00:41:32,770
And we think of this as being the direction-- say, the step

788
00:41:32,770 --> 00:41:35,610
direction.

789
00:41:35,610 --> 00:41:38,810
And we can separate the solution of the steepest descent problem

790
00:41:38,810 --> 00:41:39,710
into two pieces.

791
00:41:39,710 --> 00:41:41,070
One is computing the step size.

792
00:41:41,070 --> 00:41:42,790
The other is computing the direction.

793
00:41:45,730 --> 00:41:49,050
And this funny thing is called the dual norm.

794
00:41:49,050 --> 00:41:54,050
Every norm has a dual, which we call it dual norm,

795
00:41:54,050 --> 00:42:00,040
and it appears in this-- anyway, this is on the homework.

796
00:42:00,040 --> 00:42:04,040
And this is something that you can show.

797
00:42:04,040 --> 00:42:06,780
So there's a general formulation of the solution to the problem.

798
00:42:06,780 --> 00:42:08,200
Yeah, go ahead?

799
00:42:08,200 --> 00:42:11,560
AUDIENCE: I see the [INAUDIBLE], but there's--

800
00:42:11,560 --> 00:42:14,480
I mean, [INAUDIBLE].

801
00:42:14,480 --> 00:42:18,000
When we arrive at a certain delta w,

802
00:42:18,000 --> 00:42:21,920
there is no guarantee that the above approximation still

803
00:42:21,920 --> 00:42:24,620
holds when we plug in the delta w [INAUDIBLE].

804
00:42:24,620 --> 00:42:27,240
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: There's no guarantee that--

805
00:42:27,240 --> 00:42:31,700
AUDIENCE: That the approximation either the second or the third--

806
00:42:31,700 --> 00:42:35,120
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yes, there is no guarantee.

807
00:42:35,120 --> 00:42:36,880
Yeah, there's no guarantee about this

808
00:42:36,880 --> 00:42:39,140
at all, because I said take your loss function,

809
00:42:39,140 --> 00:42:41,840
throw away the nonlinear part, and just replace it

810
00:42:41,840 --> 00:42:43,220
with your favorite norm.

811
00:42:43,220 --> 00:42:44,840
So that doesn't sound like the kind of thing that's

812
00:42:44,840 --> 00:42:46,090
going to give you a guarantee.

813
00:42:46,090 --> 00:42:49,320
It sounds kind of random.

814
00:42:49,320 --> 00:42:51,800
But it's a framework for thinking

815
00:42:51,800 --> 00:42:54,350
about deriving different optimization algorithms.

816
00:42:54,350 --> 00:43:00,430
For the framework to actually apply, what you would want to do

817
00:43:00,430 --> 00:43:02,390
is you would want to--

818
00:43:02,390 --> 00:43:04,870
yeah, let's just talk about this because of the fact

819
00:43:04,870 --> 00:43:07,482
that we can--

820
00:43:07,482 --> 00:43:12,270
AUDIENCE: In a sense, we control how much we deviate

821
00:43:12,270 --> 00:43:15,745
from the actual weights, right?

822
00:43:15,745 --> 00:43:17,870
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yes, the larger and larger lambda

823
00:43:17,870 --> 00:43:21,670
is, somehow the better the model should be, in a sense.

824
00:43:21,670 --> 00:43:23,430
Is that what you're saying?

825
00:43:23,430 --> 00:43:25,490
Because yeah, you never move too far.

826
00:43:25,490 --> 00:43:27,850
If you make lambda infinite, you never move anywhere,

827
00:43:27,850 --> 00:43:29,600
so you're always within the linear region,

828
00:43:29,600 --> 00:43:30,910
and then the model holds.

829
00:43:30,910 --> 00:43:34,190
But it's like, you wouldn't know how big you need to make lambda

830
00:43:34,190 --> 00:43:36,670
to be for that to work.

831
00:43:36,670 --> 00:43:38,110
That's the problem.

832
00:43:38,110 --> 00:43:39,990
Exactly.

833
00:43:39,990 --> 00:43:43,150
Let's just think a little bit about how we could really

834
00:43:43,150 --> 00:43:44,802
pick such a norm.

835
00:43:44,802 --> 00:43:46,510
So you remember that-- let's just go back

836
00:43:46,510 --> 00:43:48,250
to get the Taylor expansion.

837
00:44:01,390 --> 00:44:03,490
So this is the Taylor expansion.

838
00:44:03,490 --> 00:44:05,770
Assuming that the loss function is an analytic--

839
00:44:05,770 --> 00:44:08,190
which it's not always, but let's assume it is--

840
00:44:08,190 --> 00:44:11,110
then this Taylor expansion really holds.

841
00:44:11,110 --> 00:44:13,370
But what if we could do the following thing?

842
00:44:13,370 --> 00:44:16,830
What if we could upper-bound the Taylor expansion

843
00:44:16,830 --> 00:44:23,750
with the linear piece, and we could produce a lambda

844
00:44:23,750 --> 00:44:27,270
and we could produce a norm such that this was really

845
00:44:27,270 --> 00:44:28,290
an upper bound?

846
00:44:31,270 --> 00:44:33,270
If we could produce such a lambda,

847
00:44:33,270 --> 00:44:35,190
then we could get the kind of guarantees

848
00:44:35,190 --> 00:44:37,070
that you're talking about.

849
00:44:37,070 --> 00:44:40,790
And actually, on the homework, you're going to do this.

850
00:44:40,790 --> 00:44:42,527
Although it's a bonus question, so maybe

851
00:44:42,527 --> 00:44:43,610
you're not going to do it.

852
00:44:43,610 --> 00:44:46,070
But if you want to, you can do it.

853
00:44:46,070 --> 00:44:48,630
And for the case of a linear model

854
00:44:48,630 --> 00:44:52,165
a linear predictor in the square loss.

855
00:44:52,165 --> 00:44:53,540
And then the hope of putting that

856
00:44:53,540 --> 00:44:55,453
on the homework is that you would then think,

857
00:44:55,453 --> 00:44:57,120
what if I don't have a linear predictor?

858
00:44:57,120 --> 00:44:59,000
What if I have a two-layer neural net?

859
00:44:59,000 --> 00:45:00,220
How would I do it then?

860
00:45:00,220 --> 00:45:02,240
What if I had a five-layer neural net?

861
00:45:02,240 --> 00:45:03,240
How would I do it then?

862
00:45:03,240 --> 00:45:06,380
And that's essentially what I'm trying to do in my research

863
00:45:06,380 --> 00:45:10,560
at the moment, is try to answer that question.

864
00:45:10,560 --> 00:45:14,820
Anyway, so that's the end of classical methods

865
00:45:14,820 --> 00:45:18,740
for optimization.

866
00:45:18,740 --> 00:45:22,220
And now, I want to think about, on a purely heuristic and purely

867
00:45:22,220 --> 00:45:27,620
intuitive level, how to make the training really scalable.

868
00:45:27,620 --> 00:45:30,260
And then if there's time at the end, which

869
00:45:30,260 --> 00:45:33,558
it seems like there might be, we can

870
00:45:33,558 --> 00:45:36,100
start trying to think about how to build a more formal theory

871
00:45:36,100 --> 00:45:37,400
around this type of thing.

872
00:45:40,020 --> 00:45:44,940
Does anybody have more questions?

873
00:45:44,940 --> 00:45:47,500
AUDIENCE: So for the infinity norm,

874
00:45:47,500 --> 00:45:53,410
do we update all the components of w at the same time?

875
00:45:53,410 --> 00:45:54,410
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah--

876
00:45:54,410 --> 00:45:59,127
AUDIENCE: So with the same kind of magnitude of [INAUDIBLE].

877
00:45:59,127 --> 00:46:00,210
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Exactly.

878
00:46:00,210 --> 00:46:01,950
That's why the sine function appears,

879
00:46:01,950 --> 00:46:05,390
because the sine of a vector maps a vector to another vector,

880
00:46:05,390 --> 00:46:09,090
where all the components have the same magnitude.

881
00:46:09,090 --> 00:46:12,230
AUDIENCE: Can you just repeat the questions for the--

882
00:46:12,230 --> 00:46:13,770
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Oh, sorry.

883
00:46:13,770 --> 00:46:17,570
To repeat the question, it was a question about,

884
00:46:17,570 --> 00:46:19,770
does the solution to steepest descent

885
00:46:19,770 --> 00:46:22,090
have the same magnitude update across all

886
00:46:22,090 --> 00:46:23,190
the different coordinates?

887
00:46:23,190 --> 00:46:24,390
And the answer was yes.

888
00:46:27,410 --> 00:46:29,690
AUDIENCE: Sorry, can we go--

889
00:46:29,690 --> 00:46:31,330
for the general steepest descent--

890
00:46:31,330 --> 00:46:35,610
because you decompose into step size and step direction.

891
00:46:35,610 --> 00:46:39,450
And for the step direction, if you look at the dot product,

892
00:46:39,450 --> 00:46:41,610
then the direction we're going to maximize

893
00:46:41,610 --> 00:46:43,850
is exactly the gradient direction.

894
00:46:43,850 --> 00:46:47,640
I just don't know what's the intuition behind why

895
00:46:47,640 --> 00:46:49,200
imposing the--

896
00:46:49,200 --> 00:46:52,220
imposing a constraint on the norm of your self-direction

897
00:46:52,220 --> 00:46:53,840
is going to help.

898
00:46:53,840 --> 00:46:56,840
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: So if the constraint is the--

899
00:46:56,840 --> 00:46:58,060
sorry.

900
00:46:58,060 --> 00:47:02,280
The question was, under a Euclidean norm penalty,

901
00:47:02,280 --> 00:47:04,480
the solution is to always point the step

902
00:47:04,480 --> 00:47:06,500
direction in the same direction as the gradient.

903
00:47:06,500 --> 00:47:08,340
And that's kind of an intuitive thing.

904
00:47:08,340 --> 00:47:11,160
But what seems less intuitive is if I put a different norm

905
00:47:11,160 --> 00:47:12,240
penalty.

906
00:47:12,240 --> 00:47:13,960
The optimal direction may change,

907
00:47:13,960 --> 00:47:16,920
and it may point in a different direction from the gradient.

908
00:47:16,920 --> 00:47:18,680
And the question is why?

909
00:47:18,680 --> 00:47:21,000
Why would that ever be a good thing to do?

910
00:47:21,000 --> 00:47:23,860
AUDIENCE: Yeah, [INAUDIBLE] want to consider a norm like this?

911
00:47:23,860 --> 00:47:28,200
Why would I want to impose a norm constraint on my step size

912
00:47:28,200 --> 00:47:29,360
direction?

913
00:47:29,360 --> 00:47:31,720
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: That's exactly the question--

914
00:47:31,720 --> 00:47:33,020
that's the right question.

915
00:47:33,020 --> 00:47:34,640
Why would I want to do that?

916
00:47:34,640 --> 00:47:36,440
Why would I want to impose a different norm

917
00:47:36,440 --> 00:47:38,520
other than the Euclidean norm?

918
00:47:38,520 --> 00:47:41,120
So I'm trying to say that, implicitly, by--

919
00:47:41,120 --> 00:47:43,640
I'm trying to put it upon you that, implicitly,

920
00:47:43,640 --> 00:47:47,730
when you say the best direction is the gradient direction,

921
00:47:47,730 --> 00:47:50,390
I'm trying to say that you're thinking Euclidean.

922
00:47:50,390 --> 00:47:53,190
So the question-- why would another norm

923
00:47:53,190 --> 00:47:56,430
be better, which would tell you to go in a different direction?

924
00:47:56,430 --> 00:48:02,950
Well, the rough intuition is, what if all the axes in my space

925
00:48:02,950 --> 00:48:05,710
are not equal with each other?

926
00:48:05,710 --> 00:48:10,230
Imagine you get a map of the US, and then you take the map

927
00:48:10,230 --> 00:48:11,750
and you squeeze it.

928
00:48:11,750 --> 00:48:14,830
So you get the corner in Photoshop,

929
00:48:14,830 --> 00:48:17,490
and you squeeze the corners together so it becomes very--

930
00:48:17,490 --> 00:48:22,470
and then you-- so taking a step of 1 centimeter East-West would

931
00:48:22,470 --> 00:48:25,730
correspond to moving like 10,000 miles, 2,000 miles,

932
00:48:25,730 --> 00:48:28,230
whereas taking one centimeter step North-South might

933
00:48:28,230 --> 00:48:31,550
correspond to going 10 miles just because you stretch the map

934
00:48:31,550 --> 00:48:33,070
in a weird way.

935
00:48:33,070 --> 00:48:35,750
And when you say that it's natural

936
00:48:35,750 --> 00:48:40,110
that going in the same direction as the gradient is

937
00:48:40,110 --> 00:48:42,670
the right thing to do, you're assuming that you have a very

938
00:48:42,670 --> 00:48:45,340
isotropic map of the United States,

939
00:48:45,340 --> 00:48:48,220
where taking steps of equal-- and what

940
00:48:48,220 --> 00:48:51,020
I'm trying to-- the idea is that you might not

941
00:48:51,020 --> 00:48:53,140
be so lucky that the loss function-- because

942
00:48:53,140 --> 00:48:55,460
of-- in deep learning, because of the architecture

943
00:48:55,460 --> 00:48:58,105
of the network and so on, it may not have that property

944
00:48:58,105 --> 00:48:59,980
that different directions in the weight space

945
00:48:59,980 --> 00:49:03,940
are equal to each other-- may be a very non-isotropic space.

946
00:49:03,940 --> 00:49:07,380
AUDIENCE: So you're saying that there's actually some--

947
00:49:07,380 --> 00:49:10,160
doing different norms somehow equivalent.

948
00:49:10,160 --> 00:49:12,040
I'm scaling each coordinate--

949
00:49:12,040 --> 00:49:14,540
I'm basically using a preconditioner matrix

950
00:49:14,540 --> 00:49:16,818
to scale each coordinate at a different rate?

951
00:49:16,818 --> 00:49:18,360
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: That's one example.

952
00:49:18,360 --> 00:49:21,860
Yes, so like that-- you could take a norm--

953
00:49:21,860 --> 00:49:25,460
I can say that I'm going to build this new norm.

954
00:49:25,460 --> 00:49:30,960
So remember the Euclidean norm is like sum over i wi squared.

955
00:49:30,960 --> 00:49:33,620
And I could say, well, I want to build a new norm, where

956
00:49:33,620 --> 00:49:36,100
I take positive constants, and I just

957
00:49:36,100 --> 00:49:38,620
scale the different coordinates differently.

958
00:49:38,620 --> 00:49:39,560
I could do that.

959
00:49:39,560 --> 00:49:41,860
And I could derive a steepest descent algorithm

960
00:49:41,860 --> 00:49:43,620
under this new norm, and it would

961
00:49:43,620 --> 00:49:46,460
correspond to some reshaping of the gradient.

962
00:49:46,460 --> 00:49:48,240
But that's just one example.

963
00:49:48,240 --> 00:49:50,940
And another example is the infinity norm, or the Frobenius

964
00:49:50,940 --> 00:49:52,860
norm, or the-- and they're all ways

965
00:49:52,860 --> 00:49:56,340
of measuring-- of saying that different coordinate directions

966
00:49:56,340 --> 00:49:58,500
or different directions more generally

967
00:49:58,500 --> 00:49:59,840
have different weightings.

968
00:50:04,900 --> 00:50:06,860
And maybe one more thing to say about that

969
00:50:06,860 --> 00:50:10,780
is why is it different weightings have different--

970
00:50:10,780 --> 00:50:14,700
another intuition is if I've used the linear piece,

971
00:50:14,700 --> 00:50:17,100
like the first order term, I need

972
00:50:17,100 --> 00:50:21,440
to know for how far that linear piece is valid.

973
00:50:21,440 --> 00:50:23,360
Maybe if I move too far in that direction,

974
00:50:23,360 --> 00:50:24,940
the linear piece broke down.

975
00:50:24,940 --> 00:50:29,220
And it may break down at a different rate

976
00:50:29,220 --> 00:50:30,520
going in different directions.

977
00:50:30,520 --> 00:50:32,260
So the idea of building a norm is

978
00:50:32,260 --> 00:50:35,740
to capture at what rate does the linear piece break down

979
00:50:35,740 --> 00:50:37,840
if I move in this direction or this direction.

980
00:50:37,840 --> 00:50:38,840
It may not be the same.

981
00:50:45,980 --> 00:50:50,250
So now, we're going to forget that for a little bit

982
00:50:50,250 --> 00:50:53,530
and just thinking about scaling.

983
00:50:53,530 --> 00:50:57,250
So the intuitive question that I want to ask

984
00:50:57,250 --> 00:50:59,890
is, how large should the weight updates

985
00:50:59,890 --> 00:51:01,990
be when I do gradient descent?

986
00:51:01,990 --> 00:51:04,490
And I want to think about this kind of Goldilocks step size.

987
00:51:04,490 --> 00:51:07,210
Because if it's too big, intuitively, you're

988
00:51:07,210 --> 00:51:09,470
going to break your neural network.

989
00:51:09,470 --> 00:51:11,237
And if it's too small, you're not

990
00:51:11,237 --> 00:51:13,570
going to be training very efficiently because your steps

991
00:51:13,570 --> 00:51:14,430
are too small.

992
00:51:14,430 --> 00:51:18,250
So you want to find that nice middle.

993
00:51:18,250 --> 00:51:21,103
And someone already had this observation.

994
00:51:21,103 --> 00:51:22,770
The observation is that a neural network

995
00:51:22,770 --> 00:51:24,770
is built out of matrices.

996
00:51:24,770 --> 00:51:27,690
It's not one big vector.

997
00:51:27,690 --> 00:51:32,130
The weight vector is like matrix 1, matrix 2, matrix 3.

998
00:51:32,130 --> 00:51:36,193
So can we think about that matrix structure?

999
00:51:36,193 --> 00:51:37,610
And this is where I wanted to say,

1000
00:51:37,610 --> 00:51:40,160
perhaps we could try a matrix norm.

1001
00:51:40,160 --> 00:51:43,580
And the one example was the Frobenius norm.

1002
00:51:46,640 --> 00:51:52,000
So the idea is-- oh, yeah, I forgot to say.

1003
00:51:52,000 --> 00:51:58,080
Whenever anyone talks about too big or too small,

1004
00:51:58,080 --> 00:52:01,600
you should always ask in which norm?

1005
00:52:01,600 --> 00:52:06,063
It's analogous to if someone just

1006
00:52:06,063 --> 00:52:08,480
says something's really big, you have no idea what they're

1007
00:52:08,480 --> 00:52:10,080
talking about, because you always

1008
00:52:10,080 --> 00:52:12,865
need some kind of reference, like a scale.

1009
00:52:12,865 --> 00:52:14,240
And if you look at a map, there's

1010
00:52:14,240 --> 00:52:16,800
the scale at the bottom, which says five centimeters

1011
00:52:16,800 --> 00:52:20,120
corresponds to 1 mile or something.

1012
00:52:20,120 --> 00:52:23,900
So if anyone ever says that something is big or small,

1013
00:52:23,900 --> 00:52:25,560
you should always ask, what's the scale

1014
00:52:25,560 --> 00:52:26,860
that you're measuring it on?

1015
00:52:26,860 --> 00:52:31,200
And for tensors, the notion of scale is what's the norm.

1016
00:52:31,200 --> 00:52:36,400
So that's a good thing to think about.

1017
00:52:36,400 --> 00:52:39,550
So in particular, when we say that we want the update to not

1018
00:52:39,550 --> 00:52:42,110
be too big or too small, we want to choose a norm,

1019
00:52:42,110 --> 00:52:45,110
and say it should not be too big or too small in that norm.

1020
00:52:45,110 --> 00:52:48,270
And then we're going to say a neural network is built out

1021
00:52:48,270 --> 00:52:50,390
of weight matrices.

1022
00:52:50,390 --> 00:52:52,290
Maybe we can try a matrix norm.

1023
00:52:52,290 --> 00:52:54,890
Does anyone know any other matrix norms?

1024
00:53:04,183 --> 00:53:06,350
AUDIENCE: But you can also take the largest singular

1025
00:53:06,350 --> 00:53:07,970
value that's also a norm.

1026
00:53:07,970 --> 00:53:09,150
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Exactly.

1027
00:53:09,150 --> 00:53:13,630
We call it the spectral norm, or it has also some other names.

1028
00:53:13,630 --> 00:53:15,730
Does anyone else know any other ones?

1029
00:53:28,350 --> 00:53:31,610
Just wait.

1030
00:53:31,610 --> 00:53:35,430
In fact, I think I pretty much knew just the Frobenius norm

1031
00:53:35,430 --> 00:53:38,460
until maybe three years ago.

1032
00:53:38,460 --> 00:53:41,310
So spectral norm is the largest singular value.

1033
00:53:41,310 --> 00:53:42,560
There's a bunch of other ones.

1034
00:53:42,560 --> 00:53:45,140
One is called the nuclear norm.

1035
00:53:45,140 --> 00:53:50,197
There's a general class of norms called the Schatten p-norms.

1036
00:53:50,197 --> 00:53:51,780
And just wanting to point out, there's

1037
00:53:51,780 --> 00:53:54,160
actually a lot of ways to measure how big a matrix is,

1038
00:53:54,160 --> 00:53:56,700
which kind of makes sense because it's got

1039
00:53:56,700 --> 00:53:58,025
a lot of different coordinates.

1040
00:53:58,025 --> 00:53:59,900
So there's presumably a lot of different ways

1041
00:53:59,900 --> 00:54:02,040
to measure size of a matrix.

1042
00:54:08,900 --> 00:54:11,300
This is where I want to introduce--

1043
00:54:11,300 --> 00:54:14,900
so just to summarize, we want to ask

1044
00:54:14,900 --> 00:54:16,880
how big should our weight update be?

1045
00:54:16,880 --> 00:54:19,780
And we've said, well, we kind of need to pick a norm in order

1046
00:54:19,780 --> 00:54:21,960
for that question to be a meaningful question.

1047
00:54:25,300 --> 00:54:29,740
And now, I want to introduce you to this new perspective

1048
00:54:29,740 --> 00:54:31,820
in thinking about a neural network.

1049
00:54:31,820 --> 00:54:35,970
So remember that maybe when you first

1050
00:54:35,970 --> 00:54:38,670
think about a neural network, you think about neurons,

1051
00:54:38,670 --> 00:54:41,730
and there's nodes, and they're connected by edges.

1052
00:54:41,730 --> 00:54:44,690
And then you go a bit further, and you learn a bit more.

1053
00:54:44,690 --> 00:54:46,570
And then you're like, oh, I recognize this.

1054
00:54:46,570 --> 00:54:48,490
This is a weight matrix.

1055
00:54:48,490 --> 00:54:51,390
And if I have a layer with a single output,

1056
00:54:51,390 --> 00:54:53,730
then its weights are just a vector.

1057
00:54:53,730 --> 00:54:58,650
And now, I'd start to describe my neural network as like matrix

1058
00:54:58,650 --> 00:54:59,830
multiplications.

1059
00:54:59,830 --> 00:55:02,470
And I think of this as the tensor perspective.

1060
00:55:05,170 --> 00:55:07,667
And then what I want to say is you go even further,

1061
00:55:07,667 --> 00:55:09,250
and you realize that there's this kind

1062
00:55:09,250 --> 00:55:13,010
of spectral perspective on neural networks, where

1063
00:55:13,010 --> 00:55:16,130
every weight matrix has something called a Singular

1064
00:55:16,130 --> 00:55:20,010
Value Decomposition, or an SVD, which

1065
00:55:20,010 --> 00:55:22,890
is like a generalized eigenvalue decomposition.

1066
00:55:22,890 --> 00:55:26,390
Not every matrix has eigenvalue decomposition,

1067
00:55:26,390 --> 00:55:31,250
but every matrix has a singular value decomposition.

1068
00:55:31,250 --> 00:55:35,130
And we can think about taking my neural network

1069
00:55:35,130 --> 00:55:39,850
and decomposing each matrix into its SVD.

1070
00:55:39,850 --> 00:55:41,490
I'm not saying we should actually

1071
00:55:41,490 --> 00:55:45,232
go ahead and do that to train it in its spectral representation

1072
00:55:45,232 --> 00:55:46,190
or something like that.

1073
00:55:46,190 --> 00:55:50,210
I'm just saying that mentally, you can always do that.

1074
00:55:50,210 --> 00:55:52,390
It always exists, that decomposition.

1075
00:55:55,370 --> 00:55:57,210
I just wanted to introduce-- a way

1076
00:55:57,210 --> 00:55:59,370
that I think about stable training is

1077
00:55:59,370 --> 00:56:02,410
I kind of imagine my spectral decomposition of all my weight

1078
00:56:02,410 --> 00:56:03,410
matrices.

1079
00:56:03,410 --> 00:56:07,170
And one thing I think is that probably the singular

1080
00:56:07,170 --> 00:56:10,550
values should not change too drastically from step to step.

1081
00:56:10,550 --> 00:56:11,650
That would be bad.

1082
00:56:11,650 --> 00:56:14,210
But if they change too little from step to step,

1083
00:56:14,210 --> 00:56:18,170
that would be also bad because I would be training too slow.

1084
00:56:18,170 --> 00:56:20,610
It's like, another way to think about what stable training

1085
00:56:20,610 --> 00:56:23,770
means is, maybe you don't want your spectral decomposition

1086
00:56:23,770 --> 00:56:25,910
to be going haywire when you're training,

1087
00:56:25,910 --> 00:56:29,050
and maybe you don't want it to just change a teeny tiny bit--

1088
00:56:29,050 --> 00:56:33,240
something in the middle seems good.

1089
00:56:33,240 --> 00:56:36,200
That's a thought.

1090
00:56:36,200 --> 00:56:41,880
And then as somebody mentioned, there's the spectral norm.

1091
00:56:41,880 --> 00:56:44,280
So if I want to compute, let's say the spectral norm

1092
00:56:44,280 --> 00:56:47,280
of this weight matrix, I compute its singular value

1093
00:56:47,280 --> 00:56:51,200
decomposition, and then I just take the largest singular value.

1094
00:56:51,200 --> 00:56:54,820
And that's like a way of computing the spectral norm.

1095
00:56:54,820 --> 00:56:56,440
So that's another way of measuring

1096
00:56:56,440 --> 00:57:00,120
the size of a matrix, which you'll also come across

1097
00:57:00,120 --> 00:57:01,180
in the homework.

1098
00:57:04,520 --> 00:57:07,760
So I wanted to talk a little bit about the spectral norm.

1099
00:57:07,760 --> 00:57:10,760
How do we think about this thing?

1100
00:57:10,760 --> 00:57:12,600
And I drew this picture.

1101
00:57:12,600 --> 00:57:14,400
And the idea is the spectral norm

1102
00:57:14,400 --> 00:57:18,600
tells if I feed a vector into a matrix,

1103
00:57:18,600 --> 00:57:21,080
and I get something that comes out,

1104
00:57:21,080 --> 00:57:24,680
it tells me how big the thing that comes out

1105
00:57:24,680 --> 00:57:29,280
can be given that I knew how big the thing that went in was.

1106
00:57:29,280 --> 00:57:31,070
So we can write that.

1107
00:57:31,070 --> 00:57:33,350
I'm going to write it.

1108
00:57:33,350 --> 00:57:34,930
So I'll use this notation.

1109
00:57:34,930 --> 00:57:37,990
The spectral norm of a matrix M is

1110
00:57:37,990 --> 00:57:45,550
the max over input vectors with unit L2

1111
00:57:45,550 --> 00:57:52,310
norm of the L2 norm of MV.

1112
00:57:52,310 --> 00:57:53,330
So does this make sense?

1113
00:57:53,330 --> 00:57:56,710
It says, I feed in a unit vector to the matrix,

1114
00:57:56,710 --> 00:57:58,470
and I get out a vector.

1115
00:57:58,470 --> 00:58:00,910
How large can that vector possibly be?

1116
00:58:00,910 --> 00:58:03,550
That's the spectral norm.

1117
00:58:03,550 --> 00:58:05,910
I think that that's a nice idea, because when

1118
00:58:05,910 --> 00:58:07,390
you train a neural network, you're

1119
00:58:07,390 --> 00:58:09,510
putting vectors into matrices, and matrices

1120
00:58:09,510 --> 00:58:12,390
are coming out the other end.

1121
00:58:12,390 --> 00:58:14,270
Actually, something about this norm

1122
00:58:14,270 --> 00:58:15,710
is descriptive of what's actually

1123
00:58:15,710 --> 00:58:17,510
happening during training.

1124
00:58:17,510 --> 00:58:19,285
So maybe that's a good potentially

1125
00:58:19,285 --> 00:58:21,910
that could be a good norm for us to think about when we measure

1126
00:58:21,910 --> 00:58:23,130
the size of the matrices.

1127
00:58:23,130 --> 00:58:24,010
That's the idea.

1128
00:58:27,350 --> 00:58:30,000
And so there's a fact, which I have not proved,

1129
00:58:30,000 --> 00:58:32,180
but the spectral norm is equivalent to the largest

1130
00:58:32,180 --> 00:58:34,360
singular value of the weight matrix.

1131
00:58:41,040 --> 00:58:43,072
But there's something arbitrary here.

1132
00:58:43,072 --> 00:58:44,780
And what I hope to point out as arbitrary

1133
00:58:44,780 --> 00:58:49,520
is, why would we care about the L2 norm of the inputs?

1134
00:58:49,520 --> 00:58:51,540
That was an arbitrary decision.

1135
00:58:51,540 --> 00:58:54,280
And why would we care about the L2 norm of the outputs?

1136
00:58:54,280 --> 00:58:56,340
That also seems arbitrary.

1137
00:58:56,340 --> 00:59:00,540
We can actually change this to be arbitrary,

1138
00:59:00,540 --> 00:59:04,180
let's say, LP norm here and L2 norm there.

1139
00:59:04,180 --> 00:59:05,740
And you can change this definition

1140
00:59:05,740 --> 00:59:07,460
to involve any norm on the input space

1141
00:59:07,460 --> 00:59:09,340
and any norm on the output space.

1142
00:59:09,340 --> 00:59:13,460
And then we would refer to the thing that we get as the,

1143
00:59:13,460 --> 00:59:18,660
let's say, the q to p norm on the matrix.

1144
00:59:18,660 --> 00:59:20,780
It's called inducing-- we call this inducing

1145
00:59:20,780 --> 00:59:24,100
a norm on a matrix given a norm on the input space and a norm

1146
00:59:24,100 --> 00:59:26,100
on the output space.

1147
00:59:26,100 --> 00:59:28,710
So we call this an induced operator norm.

1148
00:59:32,090 --> 00:59:34,750
And this seems kind of helpful, because in my neural net,

1149
00:59:34,750 --> 00:59:37,790
maybe I don't care about the L2 norm of my activations,

1150
00:59:37,790 --> 00:59:39,490
and maybe I don't care about the L2 norm

1151
00:59:39,490 --> 00:59:40,890
of the outputs of a layer.

1152
00:59:40,890 --> 00:59:43,410
Maybe I care about some other norm.

1153
00:59:43,410 --> 00:59:45,690
And that's where we come to this thing

1154
00:59:45,690 --> 00:59:48,770
that we're going to call the RMS to RMS operator norm.

1155
00:59:48,770 --> 00:59:54,890
I'm not sure if anyone remembers about the RMS norm?

1156
00:59:54,890 --> 01:00:00,270
Does anyone remember what RMS norm is from my other lecture?

1157
01:00:05,940 --> 01:00:08,870
It's related to the L2 norm.

1158
01:00:11,570 --> 01:00:12,910
Does anyone remember?

1159
01:00:12,910 --> 01:00:14,578
It was on one of the--

1160
01:00:14,578 --> 01:00:16,870
AUDIENCE: The norm scaled by the number of coordinates.

1161
01:00:16,870 --> 01:00:18,610
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Exactly.

1162
01:00:18,610 --> 01:00:25,010
Yeah, so we define this RMS norm of a vector v

1163
01:00:25,010 --> 01:00:29,600
to be the 1 over square root d, if d is the dimension, times

1164
01:00:29,600 --> 01:00:31,020
the 2 norm of the vector.

1165
01:00:31,020 --> 01:00:33,320
So it's just a rescaled Euclidean norm.

1166
01:00:33,320 --> 01:00:38,440
But does anyone know about-- if you know much about transformers

1167
01:00:38,440 --> 01:00:41,440
or neural architectures that people use,

1168
01:00:41,440 --> 01:00:44,920
we often make a big effort to normalize

1169
01:00:44,920 --> 01:00:48,420
all of the activations of the layer in the RMS norm.

1170
01:00:48,420 --> 01:00:51,320
And this is referred to either as RMS normalization, or layer

1171
01:00:51,320 --> 01:00:53,280
normalization, or layer norm.

1172
01:00:53,280 --> 01:00:55,160
It's a standard thing that, if you actually

1173
01:00:55,160 --> 01:00:56,277
implement a transformer--

1174
01:00:56,277 --> 01:00:57,860
I think at some point you'll do this--

1175
01:00:57,860 --> 01:00:59,440
you'll probably use layer norm.

1176
01:00:59,440 --> 01:01:01,600
So it's like I want to make the argument

1177
01:01:01,600 --> 01:01:06,440
that for a layer in a neural network, a really natural norm

1178
01:01:06,440 --> 01:01:08,600
to equip it with for an activation vector

1179
01:01:08,600 --> 01:01:12,160
is the norm, not the Euclidean norm.

1180
01:01:12,160 --> 01:01:15,600
And another way to put this is what does it mean

1181
01:01:15,600 --> 01:01:20,120
if the norm of a vector is 1?

1182
01:01:20,120 --> 01:01:27,400
If v RMS is 1, it implies that each Vi is around 1.

1183
01:01:27,400 --> 01:01:30,300
So if you normalize the vector to have unit RMS norm,

1184
01:01:30,300 --> 01:01:33,230
you're normalizing all the coordinates to be around 1,

1185
01:01:33,230 --> 01:01:34,980
which is kind of nice for neural networks.

1186
01:01:34,980 --> 01:01:37,800
It means that the feature vectors or the activation

1187
01:01:37,800 --> 01:01:41,480
vectors are all well-behaved.

1188
01:01:41,480 --> 01:01:45,240
So we go ahead and then we just induce the operator norm

1189
01:01:45,240 --> 01:01:46,700
kind of like we described.

1190
01:01:46,700 --> 01:01:50,560
So on a matrix n, we define the RMS to RMS

1191
01:01:50,560 --> 01:01:55,120
operator norm to be the max over vectors inputs such

1192
01:01:55,120 --> 01:01:57,880
that the RMS norm of the input is one.

1193
01:01:57,880 --> 01:02:00,960
So the average input size across the coordinates of the input

1194
01:02:00,960 --> 01:02:03,840
is 1.

1195
01:02:03,840 --> 01:02:08,080
And then we measure the RMS norm of the output.

1196
01:02:08,080 --> 01:02:12,862
And so this is inducing-- using a vector space with an RMS norm,

1197
01:02:12,862 --> 01:02:14,320
and then there's a matrix, and then

1198
01:02:14,320 --> 01:02:16,278
there's another vector space with the RMS norm.

1199
01:02:16,278 --> 01:02:19,680
We induce the operator norm on the matrix.

1200
01:02:19,680 --> 01:02:24,110
And that's what we call it the RMS to RMS induced operator

1201
01:02:24,110 --> 01:02:25,830
norm.

1202
01:02:25,830 --> 01:02:28,230
And as an exercise, what you can show--

1203
01:02:28,230 --> 01:02:31,630
you can prove that this is equivalent to the spectral norm,

1204
01:02:31,630 --> 01:02:33,370
but with a dimensional factor.

1205
01:02:39,190 --> 01:02:42,150
So it's just a rescaled spectral norm with a particular dimension

1206
01:02:42,150 --> 01:02:44,470
of prefactor.

1207
01:02:44,470 --> 01:02:48,670
And this is also on the second question of the homework.

1208
01:02:48,670 --> 01:02:50,013
You just need to use this.

1209
01:02:50,013 --> 01:02:51,930
You don't need to even understand necessarily.

1210
01:02:51,930 --> 01:02:53,310
You just need to be able to normalize something

1211
01:02:53,310 --> 01:02:54,250
in that norm.

1212
01:02:58,030 --> 01:02:59,470
What is the payoff?

1213
01:02:59,470 --> 01:03:01,870
Why did we spend so long talking about different norms

1214
01:03:01,870 --> 01:03:03,670
and how to define them?

1215
01:03:03,670 --> 01:03:07,870
Well, what I claim is that defining that particular norm

1216
01:03:07,870 --> 01:03:10,990
is the thing that solves the width scaling problem.

1217
01:03:10,990 --> 01:03:13,430
So once I know about that norm, and I

1218
01:03:13,430 --> 01:03:18,910
use it to normalize my training, I can fix this problem.

1219
01:03:18,910 --> 01:03:22,040
So what's the claim?

1220
01:03:22,040 --> 01:03:25,460
The claim is to remove drift in the optimal learning rate

1221
01:03:25,460 --> 01:03:35,260
as I vary the width of my neural network, I should do two things.

1222
01:03:35,260 --> 01:03:39,140
I should initialize my weight matrix at layer L

1223
01:03:39,140 --> 01:03:48,060
such that its RMS to RMS operator norm is around 1.

1224
01:03:48,060 --> 01:03:54,260
And then I should update by a delta w at layer

1225
01:03:54,260 --> 01:04:01,420
l such that the RMS to RMS operating norm of my update

1226
01:04:01,420 --> 01:04:06,580
is also around 1.

1227
01:04:06,580 --> 01:04:11,940
Intuitively, that means it--

1228
01:04:11,940 --> 01:04:14,960
the fact that I normalize the input-- the initial matrix,

1229
01:04:14,960 --> 01:04:15,460
sorry.

1230
01:04:15,460 --> 01:04:18,340
Because I normalize my initial matrix in this norm,

1231
01:04:18,340 --> 01:04:21,210
if I pass in features that are coordinate wise 1

1232
01:04:21,210 --> 01:04:23,850
or on average 1, I can only get out

1233
01:04:23,850 --> 01:04:27,030
features that are at most coordinate wise 1.

1234
01:04:27,030 --> 01:04:29,150
So that's why it's a good initialization.

1235
01:04:29,150 --> 01:04:32,530
It controls RMS norms as you move through the network.

1236
01:04:32,530 --> 01:04:36,330
And then why is it good to normalize my updates like this?

1237
01:04:36,330 --> 01:04:38,850
Because it ensures that the amount that the features

1238
01:04:38,850 --> 01:04:41,810
change by from one step to the next.

1239
01:04:41,810 --> 01:04:44,930
We call it feature learning-- or the amount that the activation

1240
01:04:44,930 --> 01:04:46,790
vectors can change from step to step

1241
01:04:46,790 --> 01:04:51,367
is controlled in precisely the same way.

1242
01:04:51,367 --> 01:04:52,950
Does anyone have questions about that?

1243
01:04:52,950 --> 01:04:55,422
I feel like I'm just--

1244
01:04:55,422 --> 01:04:57,090
yeah, sorry.

1245
01:04:57,090 --> 01:04:58,970
AUDIENCE: Oh, it seems like you're really

1246
01:04:58,970 --> 01:05:01,690
constraining the ability of the neural network

1247
01:05:01,690 --> 01:05:05,930
to be expressive by controlling exactly what values

1248
01:05:05,930 --> 01:05:07,550
the feature vector can take on.

1249
01:05:07,550 --> 01:05:11,720
So how do you do this but not lose the approximation capacity?

1250
01:05:11,720 --> 01:05:13,470
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: That's a great question.

1251
01:05:13,470 --> 01:05:15,730
So to repeat the question, it seems

1252
01:05:15,730 --> 01:05:18,280
like constraining these properties would somehow

1253
01:05:18,280 --> 01:05:20,400
damage the expressivity of the network,

1254
01:05:20,400 --> 01:05:21,860
and how do we stop that?

1255
01:05:25,160 --> 01:05:28,040
In what I just presented, we're only controlling the property

1256
01:05:28,040 --> 01:05:31,580
at initialization, and then between consecutive steps.

1257
01:05:31,580 --> 01:05:34,600
But over many steps, things can change much more.

1258
01:05:34,600 --> 01:05:36,880
So I think that that's probably why it

1259
01:05:36,880 --> 01:05:39,680
doesn't harm the expressivity.

1260
01:05:39,680 --> 01:05:41,340
Sorry, any more questions?

1261
01:05:44,680 --> 01:05:47,120
AUDIENCE: This is more like an empirical observation.

1262
01:05:47,120 --> 01:05:49,600
Like, we do the same [INAUDIBLE] more stable

1263
01:05:49,600 --> 01:05:52,662
training where they actually have similar [INAUDIBLE]?

1264
01:05:52,662 --> 01:05:54,120
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: So in some sense,

1265
01:05:54,120 --> 01:05:55,860
it's an empirical observation.

1266
01:05:55,860 --> 01:05:58,520
And actually, the second homework problem on the homework

1267
01:05:58,520 --> 01:06:01,800
is playing with it empirically.

1268
01:06:01,800 --> 01:06:04,760
But in another sense, there's a lot of theoretical things

1269
01:06:04,760 --> 01:06:07,000
to support it, the simplest of which

1270
01:06:07,000 --> 01:06:10,120
is kind of actually just like the definition of this operator

1271
01:06:10,120 --> 01:06:10,920
norm.

1272
01:06:10,920 --> 01:06:13,360
If you just think really hard about this operator norm,

1273
01:06:13,360 --> 01:06:17,190
it tells you that things have to behave

1274
01:06:17,190 --> 01:06:19,610
in a good way in a certain sense,

1275
01:06:19,610 --> 01:06:22,070
but only in terms of an upper-bound.

1276
01:06:22,070 --> 01:06:25,910
It says that the features of the particular layer cannot change

1277
01:06:25,910 --> 01:06:27,910
more than by this amount.

1278
01:06:27,910 --> 01:06:29,570
And it's nice to know that.

1279
01:06:29,570 --> 01:06:32,190
But it doesn't tell you the features definitely

1280
01:06:32,190 --> 01:06:34,110
will change by this amount.

1281
01:06:34,110 --> 01:06:36,792
This is precisely how the internals of the--

1282
01:06:36,792 --> 01:06:39,250
and there is also a theory to talk a little bit about that,

1283
01:06:39,250 --> 01:06:41,950
but it usually makes infinite width limits.

1284
01:06:41,950 --> 01:06:45,550
So I have some references on the final slide.

1285
01:06:45,550 --> 01:06:47,275
It's like an active topic at the moment,

1286
01:06:47,275 --> 01:06:48,650
and there's a lot of experiments,

1287
01:06:48,650 --> 01:06:49,900
and there are a lot of theory.

1288
01:06:49,900 --> 01:06:53,710
But it's still a not fully resolved kind of question.

1289
01:06:53,710 --> 01:06:56,690
But what I was hoping, for the purposes of the lecture,

1290
01:06:56,690 --> 01:06:58,950
is to get across that there's quite an intuitive idea

1291
01:06:58,950 --> 01:07:02,550
of just how big should things be, and in what norm

1292
01:07:02,550 --> 01:07:03,770
should I measure that?

1293
01:07:03,770 --> 01:07:07,145
And I'm trying to make this claim, which

1294
01:07:07,145 --> 01:07:08,770
is a sort of vague claim at the moment.

1295
01:07:08,770 --> 01:07:10,650
But if you're really careful about that,

1296
01:07:10,650 --> 01:07:14,350
then you can make the training much more stable.

1297
01:07:14,350 --> 01:07:16,350
AUDIENCE: Can you help provide intuition

1298
01:07:16,350 --> 01:07:17,630
for why this code is true?

1299
01:07:17,630 --> 01:07:20,510
So I'm having trouble bridging the gap

1300
01:07:20,510 --> 01:07:24,590
with why not changing the feature vector a lot

1301
01:07:24,590 --> 01:07:27,938
actually eliminates the drift [INAUDIBLE].

1302
01:07:27,938 --> 01:07:30,230
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: One way to put it is that as I change

1303
01:07:30,230 --> 01:07:33,608
the dimension of my layer, which is what we're scaling when

1304
01:07:33,608 --> 01:07:35,650
we're scaling width-- either the input dimension,

1305
01:07:35,650 --> 01:07:37,130
the output dimension, or both--

1306
01:07:39,910 --> 01:07:43,030
the RMS norm is a dimension, it's-- in some sense,

1307
01:07:43,030 --> 01:07:44,870
it's a non-dimensional norm.

1308
01:07:44,870 --> 01:07:47,630
My system is behaving well when the norm is 1.

1309
01:07:47,630 --> 01:07:50,590
That would mean that all my activations are around 1.

1310
01:07:50,590 --> 01:07:51,770
What's the alternative?

1311
01:07:51,770 --> 01:07:54,650
It's like, the alternative is either I scale the dimension,

1312
01:07:54,650 --> 01:07:57,390
and the size of each activation either grows with dimension

1313
01:07:57,390 --> 01:07:59,050
or shrinks with dimension.

1314
01:07:59,050 --> 01:08:01,070
That would be bad scaling.

1315
01:08:01,070 --> 01:08:05,670
And if you ensured that Euclidean norms were controlled,

1316
01:08:05,670 --> 01:08:07,070
and then you trace back what that

1317
01:08:07,070 --> 01:08:09,090
means for individual coordinates,

1318
01:08:09,090 --> 01:08:11,482
you would see that either the--

1319
01:08:11,482 --> 01:08:13,190
I'm not sure which way around, but either

1320
01:08:13,190 --> 01:08:15,980
the individual coordinates are growing or shrinking.

1321
01:08:15,980 --> 01:08:17,420
The nice thing about this norm is

1322
01:08:17,420 --> 01:08:20,700
it keeps the size of the individual coordinates actually

1323
01:08:20,700 --> 01:08:22,685
invariant to the width, or the amount

1324
01:08:22,685 --> 01:08:24,060
that the coordinates are changing

1325
01:08:24,060 --> 01:08:26,000
is also invariant to the width.

1326
01:08:26,000 --> 01:08:31,120
So that's the consideration.

1327
01:08:33,819 --> 01:08:35,580
AUDIENCE: And for the first position, but

1328
01:08:35,580 --> 01:08:38,673
for the second one, why are we constraining the updates

1329
01:08:38,673 --> 01:08:40,340
given that we are going to multiply them

1330
01:08:40,340 --> 01:08:42,455
by learning rate anyway?

1331
01:08:42,455 --> 01:08:44,580
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah, so the goal is the learning

1332
01:08:44,580 --> 01:08:47,060
rate should be independent of width-- the optimal learning

1333
01:08:47,060 --> 01:08:48,687
rate should be independent of width.

1334
01:08:48,687 --> 01:08:50,979
So we're trying to package all the dimension dependence

1335
01:08:50,979 --> 01:08:54,740
in the right way into the norm so that then the learning rate--

1336
01:08:54,740 --> 01:08:57,880
after I normalize the updates in that norm, the learning rate,

1337
01:08:57,880 --> 01:09:00,380
I don't need to change it with width anymore.

1338
01:09:00,380 --> 01:09:03,640
So if you don't normalize the updates in a particular norm,

1339
01:09:03,640 --> 01:09:05,620
then the learning rate itself needs to account

1340
01:09:05,620 --> 01:09:07,200
for the dimension dependence.

1341
01:09:12,850 --> 01:09:14,810
It may be clearer, because the second homework

1342
01:09:14,810 --> 01:09:16,850
problem you implement a normalized version

1343
01:09:16,850 --> 01:09:24,290
of gradient descent, and you compare two for--

1344
01:09:24,290 --> 01:09:26,270
one without an explicit normalization,

1345
01:09:26,270 --> 01:09:28,689
one with an explicit normalization.

1346
01:09:28,689 --> 01:09:30,550
So it's something maybe--

1347
01:09:30,550 --> 01:09:35,410
let's just finish the lecture, and we can talk about this more

1348
01:09:35,410 --> 01:09:38,069
The last one is depth scaling.

1349
01:09:41,410 --> 01:09:43,550
And I kind of wimped out a little bit.

1350
01:09:43,550 --> 01:09:49,569
And also it's like an open research topic, so it's OK.

1351
01:09:49,569 --> 01:09:55,130
But remember, the problem was that if I scale the depth,

1352
01:09:55,130 --> 01:09:56,810
the performance can get worse unless I'm

1353
01:09:56,810 --> 01:09:58,930
kind of careful about things.

1354
01:09:58,930 --> 01:10:04,913
And what I wanted to do is to think about this expression.

1355
01:10:04,913 --> 01:10:06,830
Maybe I should have erased that and asked you.

1356
01:10:06,830 --> 01:10:10,962
But does anyone know what this--

1357
01:10:10,962 --> 01:10:13,420
oh, wait, you can't see what I'm pointing at-- sorry-- what

1358
01:10:13,420 --> 01:10:16,080
this evaluates to?

1359
01:10:16,080 --> 01:10:18,840
Does anyone recognize that?

1360
01:10:18,840 --> 01:10:22,020
Yeah, it's e to the x.

1361
01:10:22,020 --> 01:10:22,520
Oops.

1362
01:10:25,480 --> 01:10:30,200
And so what I want to make is an analogy, where

1363
01:10:30,200 --> 01:10:32,060
this is like a compound system.

1364
01:10:32,060 --> 01:10:34,720
It's a big product of many, many, many things.

1365
01:10:34,720 --> 01:10:38,578
But I scale the internals of that thing in a nice way,

1366
01:10:38,578 --> 01:10:41,120
such that even when I take the number of terms in the product

1367
01:10:41,120 --> 01:10:46,200
to infinity, the thing doesn't blow up, and it doesn't go to 0.

1368
01:10:46,200 --> 01:10:50,400
So that's what I call that kind of well-scaled depth limit.

1369
01:10:50,400 --> 01:10:57,680
That's something that many of us know from just math, I guess.

1370
01:10:57,680 --> 01:11:01,420
And the crucial thing is that we take x--

1371
01:11:01,420 --> 01:11:03,240
we think of this as the input--

1372
01:11:03,240 --> 01:11:07,320
and we divide it by L, which is the number of terms.

1373
01:11:07,320 --> 01:11:10,130
And then we're going to take a product of L things.

1374
01:11:10,130 --> 01:11:15,170
And it's really nice that because I divide x by L,

1375
01:11:15,170 --> 01:11:17,130
this thing converges to e to the x.

1376
01:11:17,130 --> 01:11:20,730
But if I put a different factor here-- if I put L squared,

1377
01:11:20,730 --> 01:11:21,510
I take the limit--

1378
01:11:21,510 --> 01:11:23,150
I would get 0.

1379
01:11:23,150 --> 01:11:27,070
And if I put square root of L, I take

1380
01:11:27,070 --> 01:11:29,630
the limit-- it would blow up.

1381
01:11:29,630 --> 01:11:32,590
And so it's really thinking about what factor

1382
01:11:32,590 --> 01:11:36,150
you should put there so that if I take the depth, i.e,

1383
01:11:36,150 --> 01:11:39,030
the number of terms to infinity, it remains stable.

1384
01:11:39,030 --> 01:11:40,670
That's the same of consideration you

1385
01:11:40,670 --> 01:11:43,710
need to have if you want to train a residual network,

1386
01:11:43,710 --> 01:11:47,950
and you don't want the dynamics to either blow up or go to 0

1387
01:11:47,950 --> 01:11:50,430
as you take the depth infinity.

1388
01:11:50,430 --> 01:11:54,990
So just to write a little suggestion about that,

1389
01:11:54,990 --> 01:11:56,790
think of a residual network as being

1390
01:11:56,790 --> 01:11:59,350
a-- think of a single block in a residual network

1391
01:11:59,350 --> 01:12:02,830
as being a function which takes x and gives back

1392
01:12:02,830 --> 01:12:07,430
x plus the block of x.

1393
01:12:07,430 --> 01:12:09,680
And the block could be like a little MLP,

1394
01:12:09,680 --> 01:12:11,340
or it could be an attention layer.

1395
01:12:11,340 --> 01:12:14,340
It could be some other neural layer.

1396
01:12:14,340 --> 01:12:17,220
What do I want to put in front of the blocks such

1397
01:12:17,220 --> 01:12:20,460
that if I have an infinite number of these blocks,

1398
01:12:20,460 --> 01:12:22,620
it's still a nice function.

1399
01:12:22,620 --> 01:12:26,020
Probably I want to divide by the number of blocks, which I'll

1400
01:12:26,020 --> 01:12:34,540
call L. So intuitively, you can think of a residual network

1401
01:12:34,540 --> 01:12:36,920
a bit like this is one block.

1402
01:12:36,920 --> 01:12:39,460
And then I can think about raising it to the power L,

1403
01:12:39,460 --> 01:12:43,220
but that really means composing it with itself L times.

1404
01:12:43,220 --> 01:12:48,740
And you really want to be very careful about the block

1405
01:12:48,740 --> 01:12:49,720
multiplier.

1406
01:12:52,300 --> 01:12:54,800
I'm happy to talk-- there is a little more to the story,

1407
01:12:54,800 --> 01:12:56,633
so I'm happy to talk more about it if anyone

1408
01:12:56,633 --> 01:12:58,780
wants to talk about it.

1409
01:12:58,780 --> 01:13:02,020
And then I put this caveat here, because it's still--

1410
01:13:02,020 --> 01:13:04,292
it's not completely-- there's different papers saying

1411
01:13:04,292 --> 01:13:05,000
different things.

1412
01:13:05,000 --> 01:13:07,000
It's not completely the end of the story.

1413
01:13:07,000 --> 01:13:07,560
Yeah.

1414
01:13:07,560 --> 01:13:09,060
AUDIENCE: So does this have anything

1415
01:13:09,060 --> 01:13:11,960
to do with as your depth increases,

1416
01:13:11,960 --> 01:13:16,780
your complexity increases given a certain width as--

1417
01:13:16,780 --> 01:13:20,420
yeah, as your depth increases?

1418
01:13:20,420 --> 01:13:21,940
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: So the question

1419
01:13:21,940 --> 01:13:23,398
was, does this have something to do

1420
01:13:23,398 --> 01:13:25,420
with how the capacity or the complexity

1421
01:13:25,420 --> 01:13:27,360
increases as depth increases?

1422
01:13:27,360 --> 01:13:30,140
AUDIENCE: Yeah, as you increase your depth,

1423
01:13:30,140 --> 01:13:33,537
your training loss scales up.

1424
01:13:33,537 --> 01:13:35,620
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah, so what I'm saying is if--

1425
01:13:35,620 --> 01:13:40,140
what I'm trying to convey is if you set up your residual block,

1426
01:13:40,140 --> 01:13:42,000
and you're not really careful about it,

1427
01:13:42,000 --> 01:13:43,700
you can see performance gets worse

1428
01:13:43,700 --> 01:13:45,540
as you make the depth larger.

1429
01:13:45,540 --> 01:13:48,840
But I'm trying to say, here's a way to be more careful about it,

1430
01:13:48,840 --> 01:13:51,460
to try to stop that problem.

1431
01:13:51,460 --> 01:13:56,540
And if you look at a transformer code base, which I think later

1432
01:13:56,540 --> 01:13:59,100
in the class we actually do, you'll

1433
01:13:59,100 --> 01:14:03,780
see that a standard transformer block actually doesn't put a 1

1434
01:14:03,780 --> 01:14:05,890
over L multiplier.

1435
01:14:05,890 --> 01:14:09,610
The standard thing is actually to put 1 over square root L.

1436
01:14:09,610 --> 01:14:11,970
And the argument is that the blocks

1437
01:14:11,970 --> 01:14:14,330
are somehow incoherent and random with respect

1438
01:14:14,330 --> 01:14:16,230
to each other at initialization.

1439
01:14:16,230 --> 01:14:18,670
And if I add up lots of incoherent random things,

1440
01:14:18,670 --> 01:14:20,450
it's a bit like doing a random walk.

1441
01:14:20,450 --> 01:14:22,330
And a random walk moves a distance

1442
01:14:22,330 --> 01:14:25,510
like square root number of time steps in the walk,

1443
01:14:25,510 --> 01:14:29,330
so I should divide by square root that number of things.

1444
01:14:29,330 --> 01:14:31,130
So that's the rationale.

1445
01:14:31,130 --> 01:14:34,450
And it's unclear whether that's the right thing to do

1446
01:14:34,450 --> 01:14:37,490
or whether just to divide by L is the right thing to do.

1447
01:14:37,490 --> 01:14:41,570
But it's really a thing, which is part of a transformer code

1448
01:14:41,570 --> 01:14:44,550
base, is what block multiplier does your residual block have?

1449
01:14:48,073 --> 01:14:49,490
If people have more questions, I'm

1450
01:14:49,490 --> 01:14:52,390
happy to stay a little at the end and talk about them,

1451
01:14:52,390 --> 01:14:56,850
but I think I should just get to the end of the lecture.

1452
01:14:56,850 --> 01:15:01,170
So the last thing I wanted to talk about is something that

1453
01:15:01,170 --> 01:15:02,130
I'm--

1454
01:15:02,130 --> 01:15:05,120
I'm working on at the moment.

1455
01:15:05,120 --> 01:15:07,800
So again, you can be very skeptical

1456
01:15:07,800 --> 01:15:10,780
and feel free to not believe me.

1457
01:15:14,400 --> 01:15:17,800
But the way that we're thinking about scaling and, more

1458
01:15:17,800 --> 01:15:20,560
generally, optimization for neural networks

1459
01:15:20,560 --> 01:15:25,960
is the idea of wanting to build a very modular theory.

1460
01:15:25,960 --> 01:15:28,420
So I want to-- what does that even mean?

1461
01:15:32,120 --> 01:15:34,040
The problem with deep learning is

1462
01:15:34,040 --> 01:15:35,960
that there's such a zoo of architectures

1463
01:15:35,960 --> 01:15:37,840
that people want to consider-- that

1464
01:15:37,840 --> 01:15:39,880
how can you build an optimization theory that

1465
01:15:39,880 --> 01:15:41,140
covers all of them?

1466
01:15:41,140 --> 01:15:43,600
Because someone can always produce a new architecture,

1467
01:15:43,600 --> 01:15:47,000
and you're like, I have no idea how to deal with that.

1468
01:15:47,000 --> 01:15:51,240
But what if we could bake the construction of the optimization

1469
01:15:51,240 --> 01:15:55,360
theory into the process of building the architecture so

1470
01:15:55,360 --> 01:15:57,340
that whenever you build the architecture,

1471
01:15:57,340 --> 01:15:59,840
you also get an optimization theory?

1472
01:15:59,840 --> 01:16:03,310
That would be kind of cool.

1473
01:16:03,310 --> 01:16:07,010
So the idea is to build the theory with the neural network.

1474
01:16:09,870 --> 01:16:11,830
And we have this--

1475
01:16:11,830 --> 01:16:14,130
to actually formalize that and think about that,

1476
01:16:14,130 --> 01:16:15,870
we have this idea of a module.

1477
01:16:15,870 --> 01:16:17,492
And PyTorch already has modules.

1478
01:16:17,492 --> 01:16:18,950
So if you know a lot about PyTorch,

1479
01:16:18,950 --> 01:16:21,270
you can think about PyTorch module.

1480
01:16:21,270 --> 01:16:23,710
But a module just means something

1481
01:16:23,710 --> 01:16:29,950
with weights in a weight space, inputs in an input space,

1482
01:16:29,950 --> 01:16:32,690
and outputs in an output space.

1483
01:16:35,517 --> 01:16:37,350
And notice that that's an abstraction, which

1484
01:16:37,350 --> 01:16:40,310
can handle an individual layer in a neural network

1485
01:16:40,310 --> 01:16:42,130
or a full neural network.

1486
01:16:42,130 --> 01:16:46,310
They're all members of this class of things.

1487
01:16:46,310 --> 01:16:49,990
So even the ReLU, you can think of it as having an empty weight

1488
01:16:49,990 --> 01:16:51,910
space, so it's also a module.

1489
01:16:51,910 --> 01:16:54,450
But also a full transformer is also a module.

1490
01:16:57,030 --> 01:16:59,370
And the idea is that we're going to write a library.

1491
01:16:59,370 --> 01:17:03,660
Think about this as writing your own deep learning package,

1492
01:17:03,660 --> 01:17:05,940
and it's going to have a module class.

1493
01:17:05,940 --> 01:17:07,860
And then we're going to write a library

1494
01:17:07,860 --> 01:17:09,880
of what we call atomic modules.

1495
01:17:09,880 --> 01:17:17,460
And these are things like ReLU, linear, Conv,

1496
01:17:17,460 --> 01:17:20,940
like convolution, embedding.

1497
01:17:20,940 --> 01:17:23,620
These are like the basic layer types

1498
01:17:23,620 --> 01:17:25,420
that are part of the library.

1499
01:17:25,420 --> 01:17:29,660
And as you may have seen on the homework potentially,

1500
01:17:29,660 --> 01:17:32,180
you better write the forward and backward

1501
01:17:32,180 --> 01:17:35,100
functions for these things.

1502
01:17:35,100 --> 01:17:37,480
You better write those by hand for the basic things.

1503
01:17:37,480 --> 01:17:38,720
You just got to do that.

1504
01:17:38,720 --> 01:17:42,180
So the forward is the function the thing expresses,

1505
01:17:42,180 --> 01:17:46,220
and the backward is its derivatives basically.

1506
01:17:46,220 --> 01:17:47,820
But what we say is we're also going

1507
01:17:47,820 --> 01:17:50,740
to equip our module with something else, which

1508
01:17:50,740 --> 01:17:55,420
is a norm, which is going to be a function from its weight space

1509
01:17:55,420 --> 01:17:57,320
to the real numbers.

1510
01:17:57,320 --> 01:17:59,570
And we're going to say that, actually, we kind of know

1511
01:17:59,570 --> 01:18:04,730
how to do that for the basic layers.

1512
01:18:04,730 --> 01:18:07,810
As we said, for a linear layer, the good norm

1513
01:18:07,810 --> 01:18:11,730
is this RMS to RMS operator norm.

1514
01:18:11,730 --> 01:18:13,350
ReLU actually doesn't have weight,

1515
01:18:13,350 --> 01:18:14,862
so it doesn't need a norm.

1516
01:18:14,862 --> 01:18:17,570
And you could imagine that maybe we could come up with a good way

1517
01:18:17,570 --> 01:18:19,930
to equip the other layer types-- the basic layer

1518
01:18:19,930 --> 01:18:20,710
types with norms.

1519
01:18:20,710 --> 01:18:25,450
And we do that with pen and paper and by hand.

1520
01:18:25,450 --> 01:18:30,147
And then we're going to write these combination rules.

1521
01:18:30,147 --> 01:18:31,730
So we're going to think about if we've

1522
01:18:31,730 --> 01:18:33,430
got two modules from our library,

1523
01:18:33,430 --> 01:18:35,770
we're going to have a way of composing them to give us

1524
01:18:35,770 --> 01:18:38,730
a new module.

1525
01:18:38,730 --> 01:18:42,430
And so you may have thought about forward function.

1526
01:18:42,430 --> 01:18:44,770
It's obvious how to compose them.

1527
01:18:44,770 --> 01:18:47,570
It just means compose the forward functions--

1528
01:18:47,570 --> 01:18:49,190
do function composition.

1529
01:18:56,050 --> 01:18:59,050
The backward, we're just going to do the chain rule.

1530
01:18:59,050 --> 01:19:02,030
If we compose functions, we know how to compute the derivative.

1531
01:19:02,030 --> 01:19:04,330
It's like the chain rule.

1532
01:19:04,330 --> 01:19:07,930
And then the question is, how should we combine the norms?

1533
01:19:07,930 --> 01:19:09,950
And I'm just going to leave this as a question,

1534
01:19:09,950 --> 01:19:12,250
but it's something we've been thinking about.

1535
01:19:12,250 --> 01:19:17,930
So remember that module 1 has its own norm,

1536
01:19:17,930 --> 01:19:20,970
and module 2 has its own norm.

1537
01:19:20,970 --> 01:19:23,290
And then we need to find a way of combining

1538
01:19:23,290 --> 01:19:26,690
them to give us a norm on the composite

1539
01:19:26,690 --> 01:19:28,490
or the composed module.

1540
01:19:28,490 --> 01:19:30,170
And it comes back to that question

1541
01:19:30,170 --> 01:19:32,778
of how do we pick our norm?

1542
01:19:32,778 --> 01:19:35,070
Do you remember we were talking about steepest descent?

1543
01:19:35,070 --> 01:19:37,010
And the big question is, how do you

1544
01:19:37,010 --> 01:19:39,750
pick a norm that's a good match for your loss function?

1545
01:19:39,750 --> 01:19:41,730
And it seems horrendous for deep learning

1546
01:19:41,730 --> 01:19:43,980
because you can have any architecture that someone can

1547
01:19:43,980 --> 01:19:45,355
come to you with, and how are you

1548
01:19:45,355 --> 01:19:47,130
supposed to give them a norm so that they

1549
01:19:47,130 --> 01:19:48,590
can do steepest descent?

1550
01:19:48,590 --> 01:19:50,730
But what if there was an automatic way?

1551
01:19:50,730 --> 01:19:52,610
What if there was a way to assign norms

1552
01:19:52,610 --> 01:19:55,170
to the basic things in the library?

1553
01:19:55,170 --> 01:19:57,700
And then when people compose them,

1554
01:19:57,700 --> 01:20:00,560
it automatically gives them a norm on the composed thing.

1555
01:20:00,560 --> 01:20:03,080
It would solve the problem in a way.

1556
01:20:03,080 --> 01:20:06,440
So that's what we're trying to do.

1557
01:20:06,440 --> 01:20:09,920
And I'll just quickly tell you-- because someone asked--

1558
01:20:09,920 --> 01:20:13,200
you asked is that theory for, or is it

1559
01:20:13,200 --> 01:20:14,500
all just kind of heuristics?

1560
01:20:14,500 --> 01:20:16,560
And that is, and it's a range of things.

1561
